WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.200
- Okay, thank you all for joining us.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.420
It's my delight today to
introduce John T. Sidel

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.980
who is the Sir Patrick Gillam Professor

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.990
of International and Comparative Politics

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:22.840
at the London school of
Economics and Political Science.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.440
Professor Sidel holds a
BA and an MA from Yale

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.923
and received his PhD from Cornell.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.190
I pulled up his dissertation from Cornell

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.040
just to look at the acknowledgements page,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.550
and I won't go into all
the nitty-gritty details

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.550
but I was interested to
note that he's been involved

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.640
in the study of Philippines
and Southeast Asia

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.540
since at least 1985 when he
had an internship in Manila.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.840
But as he says in the
acknowledgements page

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.815
of the dissertation, he was
only able to detach himself

00:00:54.815 --> 00:00:58.320
from what he calls his
quote, adolescent adventures

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.720
in the realm of US
foreign policy, end quote,

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.180
and then become a serious
Southeast Asianist

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.940
after attending Yale, where he
then went on to become known

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.320
for his erudite critiques of the legacy

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.610
of US policy in the Philippines.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.740
So I'd like to think
that it's Yale's Council

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.600
on Southeast Asian Studies

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.450
that had a formative role in his career.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.890
As an undergraduate at Yale,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.020
he studied with Hal Conklin and Jim Scott.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.293
And then he went on to study
with Ben Anderson at Cornell.

00:01:30.492 --> 00:01:33.640
And I like to mention these
Yale to Cornell stories

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.595
because there really are several.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:38.390
Eric Tagliacozzo comes to mind.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.930
Tom Pepinsky also comes to
mind, people going from Yale

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.697
and then taking up positions at Cornell

00:01:44.697 --> 00:01:47.390
because they also remind
me of my own passage

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.020
from the other direction,
from Cornell to Yale.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.710
And they really bring up and
solidify all the connections

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.660
and mutual inspiration
we have and that we get

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.810
from each other across
this Yale-Cornell network.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.520
But I also hope that today's talk

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.780
can be part of an ongoing connection

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.980
between Yale Southeast
Asian Studies and the LSE

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:12.060
where John has been teaching since 2004.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.585
And also, so as where
he taught before that

00:02:14.585 --> 00:02:18.250
in other universities working
on Southeast Asia in the UK.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.270
I really hope that we can forge

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.110
some more transnational connections

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.183
for the study of Southeast Asian studies.

00:02:27.095 --> 00:02:27.928
John Sidel, as you know,

00:02:27.928 --> 00:02:30.500
is the author of numerous
books and articles.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.500
I've just going to mention some of them.

00:02:34.107 --> 00:02:36.430
"Capital, Coercion, and Crime"

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.137
which emerged from that dissertation,

00:02:38.137 --> 00:02:39.750
"Bossism in the Philippines,"

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.690
Stanford University Press 1999.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.567
Coauthored with Eva-Lotta Hedman is

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:48.910
"Philippine Politics and Society
in the Twentieth Century:

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.540
Colonial Legacies,
Post-colonial Trajectories,"

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.467
Routledge, 2000.

00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:58.450
"Riots, Pogroms, Jihad:
Religious Violence in Indonesia,"

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.847
Cornell Press, 2006.

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:04.100
"The Islamist Threat in
Southeast Asia: A Reassessment"

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.490
for the East-West Center in 2007

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.250
along with Jaime Faustino,
"Thinking and Working Politically

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:15.150
in Development: Coalitions
for Change in the Philippines"

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.340
published for the Asia Foundation in 2020.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.941
And of course the book we'll
be learning about today,

00:03:21.941 --> 00:03:26.941
"Republicanism, Communism, Islam:

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.730
Cosmopolitan Origins of
Revolution in Southeast Asia"

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.873
which is just out now from
Cornell University Press,

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.003
literally just off the press here in 2021.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.700
I'll stop my comments
there just so we can really

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.330
spend all our time listening
to John, but please do join me

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.530
in welcoming John Sidel
for today's discussion

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.203
of his new book, John.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.200
- Many thanks, Erik, for that
very generous introduction.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.700
I'm not sure I deserve it at all,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.229
but certainly Yale deserves my thanks

00:04:00.229 --> 00:04:03.010
and the Council for
Southeast Asian Studies

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.390
and these Brown Bag Seminars
for first drawing me

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.950
into that sense of community, that kind

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:12.950
of cosmopolitan archipelagic
network as you put it,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.613
some call it a mafia,

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.599
across Southeast Asian studies centers

00:04:17.599 --> 00:04:21.090
and universities across the world.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.210
So it's been a pleasure and a privilege.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.730
And I hope that I will honor
some of the people here,

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:31.369
actually, I've seen are
attending in terms of their work

00:04:31.369 --> 00:04:34.868
by talking about my book,
which I hope is in some ways

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:39.720
can be understood as a tribute
to a broader set of trends

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.510
in the historiography of Southeast Asia.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:47.500
So what I'd like to do
today is give you a flavor

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.467
of the book and also give
you a sense of its structure

00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:55.283
and the core argument, and
in doing so, I will now

00:04:55.283 --> 00:05:00.057
throw up some PowerPoint
slides, in large measure photos

00:05:00.057 --> 00:05:02.977
because sadly, the book
is not filled with photos

00:05:02.977 --> 00:05:07.500
as it perhaps would be better
embellished with photos,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.760
but at least this gives some of the flavor

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.939
and evokes some of the
kind of lived realities

00:05:12.939 --> 00:05:16.080
that I'm trying to evoke in the book.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.150
So there we see the title.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.910
So in terms of the flavor for
the book, I should acknowledge

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:27.250
that the book has a kind
of a seemingly rambling

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.545
narrative style in the
beginning of the chapters

00:05:30.545 --> 00:05:34.631
on the successive
revolutions that I treat.

00:05:34.631 --> 00:05:39.130
And it begins in seemingly strange places.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:43.520
So for example, the first two chapters

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:44.740
following the introduction, two chapters

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.308
on the Philippine Revolution
begin in the obscure

00:05:48.308 --> 00:05:51.350
small town of Litomerice,

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:54.580
which is perhaps 45
minutes Northwest of Prague

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:58.700
in what today is the
Czech Republic in Bohemia

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.710
and the area that Hitler
called the Sudetenland

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.900
and the Czechs call the pohranicni,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:07.900
the borderlands with Germany
and German speaking areas.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.850
And I began here in no small measure

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:18.180
because this town was, and
Bohemia more generally,

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.210
was kind of a seriously contested site

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:22.330
during the reformation
and even before that

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:26.770
in terms of Christianity and
played host for many years,

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:30.630
for centuries to a Jesuit
seminary, and it's in this context

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:35.630
of a broader orbit of what I
call Catholic cosmopolitanism

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:39.000
that we see connections
between the Philippines

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.440
and this town in the person
of Ferdinand Blumentritt,

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.700
a man who corresponded closely

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.870
and expressed great sympathies and support

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:50.410
for José Rizal, the
great Philippine novelist

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:53.220
and nationalist icon in
the late 19th century

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.620
as he faced a series of trials

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.430
and tribulations in his last years,

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.740
including his eventual
execution by the Spaniards.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:03.850
But here, although Litomerice
has its own history

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:08.850
in kind of Czech nationalist history,

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:12.720
I think what's interesting
is that Blumentritt himself

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:14.530
was no supporter of Czech nationalism.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:19.050
He was a supporter of a
German liberal tradition

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:22.976
and envisaged and supported
a German liberal future

00:07:22.976 --> 00:07:27.689
for Bohemia as part of a
broader German speaking kind

00:07:27.689 --> 00:07:31.405
of context, as we know from
the Austro-Hungarian empire.

00:07:31.405 --> 00:07:36.220
And yet his background and
his connections to Rizal

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.770
can be understood within
this broader orbit

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:43.500
of Catholic cosmopolitanism
that's so deeply rooted

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.710
in that setting that the Czech
saint, the Bohemian Saint

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.953
Jan Nepomucky, 14th
century saint is a sort

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:58.350
of common feature of many
sort of municipal plazas

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.810
and churches and of public
life in the Philippines

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.410
as in many other parts of the world known

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:06.740
as San Juan Nepomuceno, and in fact

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:10.870
one of the most prominent
political families in the province

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:15.870
of Pampanga that I recall
encountering 1987 when the uncle

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:17.880
of this fellow was assassinated.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.190
The Nepomucceno family they're
a wealthy, powerful family

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:23.200
with a series of of supermarkets,

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.280
Nepo Marts around the province.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:28.957
So there are traces of
Bohemian Catholicism

00:08:28.957 --> 00:08:33.957
in the Philippines that go far
beyond the Jesuit seminary,

00:08:34.406 --> 00:08:37.220
the gymnasium where Blumentritt taught

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:40.260
and the correspondence
between him and Rizal.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.467
It's no coincidence as it were

00:08:42.467 --> 00:08:44.610
in a similar but different vein,

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.260
I begin the five chapters
on the Indonesian Revolution

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.790
in the early 20th century oil boom context

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:54.180
of the city of Baku, today the capital

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:58.930
of the Azerbaijan Republic,
which in the early 20th century

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:02.140
was notable for a number
of distinctive features.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.278
On the one hand, given the oil boom

00:09:04.278 --> 00:09:07.570
it was one of the most
industrialized towns

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:10.470
or cities in the Muslim
world and in this way,

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:14.160
resembled Indonesia with its early forms

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.480
of industrialized wage, labor,
and industrial production,

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.010
especially in those areas
connected to port harbors

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:26.173
through railroads, linked up
to the great sugar plantations

00:09:26.173 --> 00:09:29.900
of Java and parts of Sumatra at the time.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:34.570
And Baku was like Indonesia by
the turn of the 20th century,

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:39.160
a hub for new forms of Islamic modernism

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:42.255
and expression and activism.

00:09:42.255 --> 00:09:46.824
For example, the political
party Musavat that emerged

00:09:46.824 --> 00:09:50.100
in the wake of the sort
of very tumultuous period

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:52.898
of World War I, but it was also a hub

00:09:52.898 --> 00:09:55.970
for revolutionary socialism.

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:59.080
So Joseph Stalin, as we know him today,

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:02.132
spent a great deal of his
time in the early 1900s

00:10:02.132 --> 00:10:06.074
as a kind of gangster
kidnapper revolutionary

00:10:06.074 --> 00:10:10.462
and Lothario in Baku.

00:10:10.462 --> 00:10:14.580
And we know that in the
aftermath of World War I

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:15.910
after a brief experiment

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:20.313
with an independent Azerbaijan
Republic that we find Baku

00:10:20.313 --> 00:10:24.870
as a hub for Bolshevik propaganda

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:27.260
within and beyond the Russian Empire

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:30.800
with the 1920 Congress of
the Peoples of the East.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:33.140
And what we can see here
that's quite interesting

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.760
is an effort to combine the
energies and aspirations

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:40.620
that we associate with revolutionary
socialism and communism

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:42.930
on the one hand and what was then

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:45.890
termed pan-Islam on the other.

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:50.890
At this Congress, the
Ukrainian Jew Grigory Zinoviev

00:10:51.056 --> 00:10:54.919
calling for a jihad against,
that's the word he used,

00:10:54.919 --> 00:10:58.115
against British and French imperialism.

00:10:58.115 --> 00:11:01.853
Something that was taken up in the context

00:11:01.853 --> 00:11:06.853
of growing awareness of
struggles among Muslim peoples

00:11:09.353 --> 00:11:13.230
in far-flung places, far from Baku

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:17.310
that might be connected up
through communism and Islam.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.060
And although there were no
representatives from Indonesia

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.180
at the time in 1920,
there were representatives

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.610
of the Communist Party of
Holland who would go on

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:29.080
to play an important role
in Indonesian history

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:31.040
and in the making of communism there.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.060
And at the Congress, there
was a poem that was read out

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:39.530
slightly adulterated by the
Acmeist poet Sergei Gorodetsky

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:41.250
about the coffee plantations

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:44.110
and the exploitation of
plantation workers in Java.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:47.700
So there was already in the
air some interest in Indonesia.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.750
And indeed two years later

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:54.480
when they held a similar
Comintern Congress in Moscow

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.601
they invited Tan Malaka who had risen

00:11:58.601 --> 00:12:02.280
to take over the Communist
Party of Indonesia

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:05.640
founded in 1920, and he spoke briefly

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:09.140
about the possibilities
of linking up communism

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:11.610
and pan-Islam as it was then called

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:14.300
before he was kind of rudely interrupted.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.910
And if you read the stenographic notes,

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:20.400
you'll see he was kind of
forcibly basically shut up

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:24.060
and forced off the stage
as this was not convenient.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:29.060
And just as he became subsequently
marginalized, likewise,

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:33.160
the the local head of the
so-called Muslim Commission

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:37.780
within the People's
Commissariat for Nationalities

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:41.990
headed by Stalin, Mirsaid
Sultan-Galiev with his aspirations

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:46.210
for Muslim communism disappeared,
not only marginalized

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.900
but eventually imprisoned in the gulags

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:51.300
and executed years later.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:55.730
So we can see in Baku
the early possibility

00:12:55.730 --> 00:13:00.730
but then the failed kind of consummation

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:05.110
of a possible alliance between
forces acting in the name

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.980
of revolutionary socialism
or communism on the one hand

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:10.230
and Islam on the other,
something that's crucial

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:13.230
for understanding the
Indonesian Revolution.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.610
And finally, if we take
the Vietnamese revolution,

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:18.440
which I treat in the final three chapters

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:20.140
of the book before the conclusion,

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:21.893
I begin much more predictably

00:13:21.893 --> 00:13:25.120
in the Southern Chinese city of Guangzhou,

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.850
which is often referred
to in the 19th centuries

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:30.870
and earlier as Canton in Western sources.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:35.300
And this is probably more
understandable or obvious

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:40.300
as a move given the close connections

00:13:40.314 --> 00:13:44.210
that I'll talk about
between what becomes Vietnam

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.720
and Guangzhou in Southern
China more generally,

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.500
but it's also understandable
in the sense that Guangzhou was

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:50.950
as some have described it,

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:53.450
the cradle of the revolution in China.

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:57.830
It's after all, the city from
which or at least around which

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:00.620
we see the great luminaries
of the late 19th centuries,

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.720
Chinese intellectual revolutions

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.043
Kang Youwei and Liang Qichao.

00:14:05.043 --> 00:14:09.943
If we think of Sun Yat-sen,
he comes from a family

00:14:09.943 --> 00:14:14.890
that immigrates from Guangdong Province

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:16.816
and from the Pearl River Delta.

00:14:16.816 --> 00:14:19.285
And he ends up back in Guangzhou

00:14:19.285 --> 00:14:21.480
heading up a nationalist government

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:24.410
after the first Chinese
Revolution that overthrew

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:28.840
the Qing dynasty, such
that when Ho Chi Minh,

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:33.420
as we now know him, arrived
in Guangzhou in 1924

00:14:34.587 --> 00:14:38.020
he found it, the city as
the hub and the capital

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:41.750
of the nationalist government
trying to reclaim China,

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:45.220
but also as a hub for
various forms of experiments

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:46.578
with radical politics.

00:14:46.578 --> 00:14:50.170
It's in Guangzhou where you see

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:55.150
the Hong Kong-Canton Seamen's
Strike being carried out

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:57.384
with sympathy and support and solidarity.

00:14:57.384 --> 00:15:00.990
It's in Guangzhou that we
see the short-lived commune

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:05.410
after the breakdown of the Kuomintang

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:09.290
Chinese Communist Party Alliance in 1927.

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:14.190
It's in Guangzhou that we see the Whampoa

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:16.690
or Huangpu Military Academy

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:20.730
with Soviet advisors training
nationalists and communists

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:25.730
and some Vietnamese military
cadets for military training

00:15:27.152 --> 00:15:29.350
in ways that proved crucial

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:32.230
for the Chinese and
Vietnamese revolutions.

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:36.030
And it's also, interestingly
enough, around Guangzhou

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:39.360
that we see the first Chinese
Communist Party experiments

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:42.080
with peasant organizing
under the leadership

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:43.920
of a man named Peng Pai

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:45.730
with the Peasant Movement
Training Institute

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:49.540
where Mao Zedong first began to articulate

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:54.003
and undertake peasant organizing
intellectual activities,

00:15:54.003 --> 00:15:57.740
something that Vietnamese
activists were drawn into as well.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:00.080
So it's pretty obvious why
I would start in Guangzhou.

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:02.940
And so I'm perhaps sounding
a little bit more sensible

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:07.120
for Southeast Asianists, but
here just to confuse things

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:09.410
a bit, I also include in the introduction

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:11.220
to these Vietnamese chapters,

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.900
the chapters in the Vietnamese
Revolution a discussion

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.640
of the city once known as Tananarive,

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.600
now as Antananarivo, the
capital of Madagascar

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:25.030
because it's worth noting
that in the early aftermath

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:30.030
of World War I or during
World War I itself

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:33.780
the 100,000 Vietnamese
soldiers and workers

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:35.610
who are drawn to France and Europe

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:38.320
to support the French war
effort, find themselves

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:42.460
with hundreds of thousands
of more African soldiers

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:44.520
and workers, and some Chinese soldiers

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:46.860
and workers as well, or at least workers.

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:50.720
And they mix and mingle with
them and get drawn into forms

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:55.720
of political activism that are
significant and are enabling

00:16:56.210 --> 00:16:59.760
and encouraging forms of
activism that take on a life

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:02.867
of their own in other parts
of the world, as we'll see.

00:17:02.867 --> 00:17:07.410
And there's a kind of reprise
of this linkage in 1947,

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.775
really the darkest, most difficult year

00:17:09.775 --> 00:17:13.070
for the Viet Minh as
they're fighting the French.

00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:15.980
And it's happily enough for them the year

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:18.524
where there's an
insurrection in Madagascar

00:17:18.524 --> 00:17:22.955
that distracts French troops
and draws French troops away

00:17:22.955 --> 00:17:26.679
to put down very brutally
and violently an insurrection

00:17:26.679 --> 00:17:31.679
in this African Island and
French colony at the time.

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:34.504
So in other words, I'm trying to evoke

00:17:34.504 --> 00:17:39.504
the kind of entanglement
of Vietnam's revolution

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:42.950
with a broader arc of
developments and trends

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:45.306
that extend not only to neighboring China,

00:17:45.306 --> 00:17:49.540
no big news there, perhaps,
but also extrapolating a bit

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:52.860
in terms of an African connection as well.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:57.860
Okay, so, so far so confusing
and so far from Southeast Asia

00:17:58.550 --> 00:18:01.180
but just to reassure you,
there's a kind of method

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.790
to the madness, and much of the action

00:18:03.790 --> 00:18:06.830
and much of the point really
has to do with Southeast Asia.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:08.907
I can promise you that.

00:18:08.907 --> 00:18:13.907
And here, I think the argument
that I'm making is framed

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:18.944
for this audience and
for the book as a whole,

00:18:18.944 --> 00:18:23.100
not for scholars of revolution,
not for political scientists

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.640
but for people interested
in Southeast Asian studies.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:28.450
And it's framed in the
context of what seemed to me

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:32.060
to be two crucial distinctive features

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:33.730
of Southeast Asian studies,

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:37.830
especially in that kind
of Cornell-Yale nexus

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:42.830
or axis or corridor that
Erik was mentioning.

00:18:42.939 --> 00:18:44.720
And it seems to me that the
two distinctive features

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:47.050
of Southeast Asian
studies that I'm writing

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:51.516
in the context of and
in some measure against

00:18:51.516 --> 00:18:56.516
are as follows, first of
all a tendency to kind of,

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:07.530
what's the word, a kind of
preoccupation with the notion

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:12.160
of the autonomy and in John Smail's terms,

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:16.480
an authenticity of Southeast
Asia and Southeast Asians

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:21.480
rather than an understanding
of Southeast Asia as a kind of,

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:25.230
you know, poor man's or
poor woman's kind of version

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:29.270
of a bigger, better
civilization next door,

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:32.133
whether in India or in China.

00:19:33.077 --> 00:19:36.365
In other words, a notion that in order

00:19:36.365 --> 00:19:40.030
to study Southeast Asia, we don't want

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:43.720
to be second-rate,
third-rate Orientalists.

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:47.610
We want to claim that
there's something distinctive

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.170
about Southeast Asia, and
you can't understand it

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:54.599
if you just think that
it's a cheap imitation

00:19:54.599 --> 00:19:56.920
of something from beyond.

00:19:56.920 --> 00:19:59.770
And this preoccupation
is evident if we think

00:19:59.770 --> 00:20:04.130
of O.W. Wolters' famous
account of Khmer Hinduism

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:06.470
where he tells us that Khmer Hinduism

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:08.720
is as Khmer as it is Hindu.

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:12.860
Clifford Geertz's account
of religion on Java,

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:16.730
that Islam is as Javanese as
it is anything else on Java,

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:20.920
and Vince Rafael's account
of what's gained and lost

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:25.250
in the translation of Spanish Catholicism

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:29.240
and Latin Roman Catholicism into Tagalog

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:33.230
in the early period of
Spanish colonial rule

00:20:33.230 --> 00:20:36.970
in the Philippines in the
16th and 17th centuries.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:40.060
So this is one kind of
preoccupation that I think

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:43.130
is very striking about
Southeast Asian studies.

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:46.610
And the second is a
preoccupation with nationalism.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:50.610
And here I'm about a preoccupation
that's there very much

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:55.610
in the '60s and '70s and
'80s in the Cold War era

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.060
in which scholars of Southeast Asia

00:20:58.060 --> 00:21:02.980
opposed to US involvement in Indochina,

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:07.140
opposed to various forms of
US intervention in the region

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:12.140
are understandably
concerned to argue and show

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:18.790
how various forms of
anti-imperialism in Southeast Asia

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:23.370
are reflective of authentic
forms of nationalism,

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:26.810
rather than just a bunch
of fellow travelers

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:29.765
or communists in disguise.

00:21:29.765 --> 00:21:34.100
And so there's a great deal
of energy and effort devoted

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:38.940
to the analysis of
nationalism in Southeast Asia.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:42.410
And it's in this context,
this Cold War context,

00:21:42.410 --> 00:21:47.110
it seems to me, that we see
the production and publication

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:50.480
by Benedict Anderson of the
single most interesting,

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:55.340
important, and insightful
book that we have

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:58.240
about nationalism in general,
"Imagined Communities."

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:02.350
And it seems to me that since
the publication of this book

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:05.848
in 1983, it's worth
noting, with opening pages

00:22:05.848 --> 00:22:09.510
that really emphasize the Cold War context

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.570
in which it's written, the
so-called Second Cold War

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:16.167
under Ronald Reagan
before Mikhail Gorbachev

00:22:16.167 --> 00:22:21.167
assumes office in Moscow
that we see the creation

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:22.870
of a kind of template

00:22:22.870 --> 00:22:25.300
for understanding Southeast Asian history,

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:29.410
a template that came to
be dominant by the end

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:32.290
of the 20th century in the
study of Southeast Asia.

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:35.760
And this template sort
of works as follows,

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:40.760
fairly clearly and coherently,
and here concisely, namely

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:45.192
that we see from the relative
incoherence and fuzziness

00:22:45.192 --> 00:22:48.420
of loosely structured dynastic realms

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:50.860
up into the 19th century,

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:54.360
we see with the rise of
modern, mostly colonial states

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:57.030
in the late 19th and early 20th century

00:22:57.030 --> 00:22:59.150
the creation of administrative structures

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:02.420
and then parallel school
systems and languages

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:07.420
of state that enable the
emergence and expansion of forms

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:11.730
of shared experience,
expression, understanding,

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:13.830
self-understanding, and consciousness

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:17.010
that are proto-nationalist and enable

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:20.630
and impel nationalist
forms of mobilization

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:24.640
and then give rise to new nation states

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:28.000
by the mid-late 20th century.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.180
And so this creates for us

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:33.330
and has established quite conveniently

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:36.173
for people teaching
Southeast Asian history

00:23:36.173 --> 00:23:39.930
a kind of neat template for understanding,

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:42.900
a kind of teleology for understanding

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:45.860
modern Southeast Asian history.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:47.993
- But this template, it seems to me,

00:23:47.993 --> 00:23:52.240
raises two questions
which can't be answered

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:56.260
within its own terms,
within this template itself.

00:23:56.260 --> 00:24:00.670
First of all, how can
we explain the variance

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:04.020
that we observe in the
pathways to independence?

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:07.460
Especially revolutions on the one hand

00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:09.610
in their different forms,
in different timing,

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:13.567
different varying success on the one hand

00:24:13.567 --> 00:24:17.160
and on the other much
more staged managed forms

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:20.941
of de-colonization leading
to something more closely

00:24:20.941 --> 00:24:25.941
like neo-colonial forms
of pseudo independence.

00:24:27.008 --> 00:24:30.010
So for example, Lee Kuan Yew
famously derided Malaysia

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:33.710
as having one independence
because they were delivered it

00:24:33.710 --> 00:24:36.740
on a silver platter by British royalty

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:40.090
with a bed of roses rather
than fighting for it

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:42.410
in a kind of heroic fashion.

00:24:42.410 --> 00:24:45.580
Sharp contrast with Vietnam also.

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:49.891
But then secondly, and probably
even more problematically,

00:24:49.891 --> 00:24:53.120
or more obviously problematic
even for historians

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:58.120
rather than these damn
political scientists, is this.

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:01.820
When and where we do see mass mobilization

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:07.060
and revolutionary mass
mobilization, how can we explain it

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:11.410
when Anderson has told
us that the broad mass

00:25:11.410 --> 00:25:15.070
of the population couldn't
possibly be nationalists

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:18.570
at the time of revolutionary
mobilization, right?

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:22.380
I mean, although Anderson's
book tells us that nationalism

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:24.680
is interesting and
important, it also tells us

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:27.611
that there's a hell of a lot
less nationalism historically

00:25:27.611 --> 00:25:29.680
among far fewer people

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:32.210
than we otherwise previously
would have imagined.

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:34.955
The people who are
nationalists are going to look

00:25:34.955 --> 00:25:37.750
like these men, they're gonna be male.

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:39.040
They're gonna be educated.

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:41.440
And in the Southeast Asian contexts

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:44.190
they are basically the kind of dandies

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:49.190
and flaneurs who we associate
with a certain social class

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:53.503
because it's only these kinds
of mostly men who by the turn

00:25:53.503 --> 00:25:58.503
of the 20th or the mid-20th
century will be Andersonian

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:02.457
in terms of their education
and socialization and immersion

00:26:02.457 --> 00:26:06.120
in those circuitries of
nationalist socialization

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:10.191
and consciousness that he
tells us are so necessary

00:26:10.191 --> 00:26:13.053
for people to be nationalists
in the first place.

00:26:13.053 --> 00:26:16.690
So what about the
so-called unwashed masses?

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:20.070
The broad mass of the population
that didn't go to school,

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:22.370
that remained illiterate, unsocialized

00:26:22.370 --> 00:26:25.330
didn't see the maps on
the school room walls

00:26:25.330 --> 00:26:28.270
or circulate through those circuitries

00:26:28.270 --> 00:26:32.070
of the colonial state, what about them?

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:34.620
How did they get mobilized at all?

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:36.174
How was it possible?

00:26:36.174 --> 00:26:38.430
And here, it seems to me
that there's a tendency

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:43.430
in the literature to kind
of link and kind of marry

00:26:44.116 --> 00:26:49.116
the kind of Andersonian
elite account with a kind

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:54.410
of account of mass
participation that harks back

00:26:54.410 --> 00:26:58.710
to the first distinctive feature
of Southeast Asian studies

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:02.360
I mentioned earlier, namely what we see

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:05.580
in Reynaldo Ileto's book
"Pasyon and Revolution"

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:08.390
where he suggests that there
were distinctively Tagalog

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:12.340
or Filipino conceptions
of Kalayaan or freedom

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:17.340
which animated the
participation in the Katipunan

00:27:17.986 --> 00:27:19.890
in the Philippine Revolution.

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:21.997
Or in Ben Anderson's earlier book,

00:27:21.997 --> 00:27:25.960
"Java in a Time of Revolution"
with its great cover here.

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:30.960
Some of you might recognize
Amrih Widodo in the photo there.

00:27:31.047 --> 00:27:36.000
And here Anderson, he's talking about

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:39.380
how a distinctly Javanese
conception of power

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:42.720
informed the Pemuda,
the youth who were drawn

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:46.060
into participation and leadership

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:49.090
in sort of spearheading the Revolution.

00:27:49.090 --> 00:27:52.560
Or we can take in the case of
Vietnam, this wonderful quote

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:56.847
from Alexander Woodside in his book,

00:27:56.847 --> 00:27:59.600
"Community and Revolution
in Modern Vietnam"

00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:01.940
claiming that no amount
of statistics, rhetoric,

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:04.010
or social science theory can explain

00:28:04.010 --> 00:28:06.160
the Vietnamese revolution adequately

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:09.350
if its properties of acute
historical consciousness

00:28:09.350 --> 00:28:12.870
and cultural pride are
insufficiently considered.

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:16.820
So in other words, that
it was this deep sense

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:21.260
of shared Vietnamese identity
and culture and history

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:24.760
and community that
animated mass participation

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:28.380
among ordinary Vietnamese, peasants,

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:31.002
and participants in the revolution.

00:28:31.002 --> 00:28:35.716
But it seems to me that
these sorts of accounts

00:28:35.716 --> 00:28:40.716
and their linkage to a kind
of Andersonian elite account

00:28:40.778 --> 00:28:45.030
is inadequate or problematic in terms

00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:48.280
of where it leaves us
descriptively and analytically.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:51.590
In descriptive terms,
it raises the question

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:53.710
of what Jim Scott used to call

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:55.300
the revolution within the revolution.

00:28:55.300 --> 00:29:00.150
How is it that the commissars
managed the peasants

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:01.130
in these revolutions?

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:06.010
How is it that the miscommunications
and misrepresentations

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:09.540
in this kind of split level
of revolution in which elites

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:12.750
and masses are thinking in such
different terms was managed?

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:14.860
Clearly very problematic.

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:18.430
It also raises questions
in explanatory terms.

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:22.313
How then could we explain
the variance that we see

00:29:22.313 --> 00:29:25.460
in which some revolutions
unfold and others don't

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:29.070
and some revolutions
head in some directions

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:32.430
and assume some forms and
other take different ones?

00:29:32.430 --> 00:29:35.530
And finally, I think, a recent book

00:29:35.530 --> 00:29:39.210
by someone who I saw is in
the audience, Christian Lentz,

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:43.080
on Dien Bien Phu, the famous battle

00:29:44.418 --> 00:29:46.300
that is kind of a signifier

00:29:46.300 --> 00:29:51.300
for anticolonial nationalist,
independent struggles.

00:29:51.478 --> 00:29:53.800
He shows in his dissertation

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:58.439
and then in his recently
published book that in fact

00:29:58.439 --> 00:30:02.680
the thousands of Coolie laborers

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:04.504
who played such a crucial role

00:30:04.504 --> 00:30:07.810
in making the Vietnamese Communist Party,

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:10.080
the Viet Minh's victory possible

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:13.230
alongside the participation
of Chinese military advisers

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:17.050
and military assistance,
weren't even ethnic Vietnamese

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:19.708
or Vietnamese speakers themselves.

00:30:19.708 --> 00:30:22.683
And they are whom we would describe

00:30:22.683 --> 00:30:26.260
as ethnic minorities today instead.

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:30.350
So in other words, this
picture of this template

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:34.990
that I've suggested leaves
some key questions unanswered.

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:39.990
And it's in the kind of
go-to response and supplement

00:30:43.733 --> 00:30:48.733
and corrective to this kind
of a gap in the template,

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:53.079
it seems to me is also inadequate.

00:30:53.079 --> 00:30:55.287
So against this backdrop the book,

00:30:55.287 --> 00:30:58.930
"Republican, Communism,
Islam" as the title suggests

00:30:58.930 --> 00:31:00.890
offers a different template.

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:05.150
Not a contradictory template,
but a complimentary template.

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:10.150
And one that's not by any
stroke of imagination original,

00:31:10.270 --> 00:31:12.910
but it's rather a composite synthesis

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:19.240
of at least three decades of
scholarship by historians,

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:22.453
some of whom are graduates
of Yale University.

00:31:23.360 --> 00:31:26.540
I can see there's Kevin
Fogg in the audience,

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:30.625
and his recently published
book, Rianne Subijanto

00:31:30.625 --> 00:31:35.240
who's a PhD and
soon-to-be-published book are,

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:38.750
these are important
works in a broader trend

00:31:38.750 --> 00:31:42.680
in the literature, which
tells us something different.

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:45.100
And if you add them all
up beginning with perhaps

00:31:45.100 --> 00:31:48.499
Chris Gosha's work on Vietnam,

00:31:48.499 --> 00:31:53.499
Mike Laffan's work on
Indonesia, you begin to see

00:31:53.535 --> 00:31:56.190
a different kind of
picture coming into view.

00:31:56.190 --> 00:31:59.460
And I'm really just drawing
together these strands

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:02.150
into a composite picture

00:32:02.150 --> 00:32:04.533
with some comparative analysis as well.

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:08.620
And here, the emphasis is not
on the rise of modern states

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:10.987
and the emergence of nationalism

00:32:10.987 --> 00:32:14.700
but instead on long-distance commerce,

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:16.418
capitalist development,

00:32:16.418 --> 00:32:20.750
and cosmopolitan connections
and connectedness.

00:32:20.750 --> 00:32:24.350
And so we see up into the early modern era

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:27.680
that rising long-distance trade gives rise

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:30.370
to various forms of cosmopolitanism.

00:32:30.370 --> 00:32:33.070
There's the so-called Sanskrit Cosmopolis

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:35.200
that Sheldon Pollock writes about.

00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:39.680
There's the Arabic Cosmopolis
that Ronit Ricci writes about.

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:44.265
There's so-called
Sinographic cosmopolitanism

00:32:44.265 --> 00:32:46.671
for Vietnam in particular.

00:32:46.671 --> 00:32:50.080
there is obviously Islamic cosmopolitanism

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:52.610
beyond Arabic cosmopolitanism,

00:32:52.610 --> 00:32:57.610
and there is also a Catholic
cosmopolitanism as well

00:32:58.130 --> 00:33:00.520
through the early modern era.

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:02.870
And what I suggest in the
book is that the legacies

00:33:02.870 --> 00:33:06.440
of these forms of cosmopolitanism
are in some measure

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:09.750
determinant of the
trajectories and outcomes

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:12.360
of the revolutionary
mobilizations that we see

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:14.597
in Southeast Asia centuries later

00:33:14.597 --> 00:33:17.520
at the turn of the 20th
century in the Philippines

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:21.630
and in the mid-20th century
in Indonesia and Vietnam.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:25.170
But really it's the transportation

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:27.907
and communication revolutions
of the mid-late 19th century

00:33:27.907 --> 00:33:31.050
and the deepening
integration of Southeast Asia

00:33:31.050 --> 00:33:33.939
into the world economy
in the mid 20th century

00:33:33.939 --> 00:33:37.277
that overshadows the
older cosmopolitanisms

00:33:39.410 --> 00:33:41.723
and overlays them with new ones

00:33:41.723 --> 00:33:44.410
in concrete institutional forms

00:33:44.410 --> 00:33:48.270
which provide infrastructures
for mobilization.

00:33:48.270 --> 00:33:51.070
So it's during this period
that we see the emergence

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:55.325
of Southeast Asian cities,
their linkage up to market towns

00:33:55.325 --> 00:33:59.644
and the creation of market societies.

00:33:59.644 --> 00:34:03.720
And crucially, the tightening
integration of Southeast Asia

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:06.980
into the world economy
doesn't just couple colonies

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:10.250
and metropoles into ever more intimate,

00:34:10.250 --> 00:34:15.250
intense dyadic embraces
but opens the flood gates

00:34:15.650 --> 00:34:17.430
to more diverse influences.

00:34:17.430 --> 00:34:22.330
In the Philippine archipelago,
the port cities are opened

00:34:22.330 --> 00:34:27.330
and German and British
and other firms come in

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:30.750
that dwarf Spanish commerce.

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:34.180
In the Dutch East Indies,
many of the big plantations

00:34:34.180 --> 00:34:36.630
on Java and Sumatra are not Dutch owned,

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:40.046
but owned by other European companies.

00:34:40.046 --> 00:34:45.046
And Singapore remains an
important hub for Indies trade

00:34:45.060 --> 00:34:47.540
up until the late 19th century.

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:51.355
If we take the case of French Indochine,

00:34:51.355 --> 00:34:56.355
much of the commodities flow
to, especially rice flow

00:34:58.040 --> 00:35:02.540
to Southern China, and
some of the profits flow

00:35:02.540 --> 00:35:07.540
into French Imperial investments
in various parts of Africa.

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:13.490
The mercantile classes that
emerge in Southeast Asia

00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:18.450
are of course in considerable
measure of immigrant stock

00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:23.330
whether Chinese from Fujian
and Guangdong provinces,

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:26.860
Indian like the Chettiars, or Arab

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:30.940
from the Hadhramaut region
of what today is Yemen.

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:34.643
So in other words, what we see alongside

00:35:34.643 --> 00:35:37.930
the kind of Andersonian nationalizing

00:35:37.930 --> 00:35:40.160
or proto-nationalizing trends

00:35:40.160 --> 00:35:45.160
is something very
cosmopolitan in its forms.

00:35:45.580 --> 00:35:49.254
And we can see this in
the distinctively hybrid

00:35:49.254 --> 00:35:53.300
and cosmopolitan forms
of popular entertainment,

00:35:53.300 --> 00:35:57.687
popular opera, popular
forms of entertainment

00:36:00.980 --> 00:36:03.530
that we see in the cities
and towns of Southeast Asia.

00:36:03.530 --> 00:36:06.500
The Komedi Stamboel in the Indies

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:08.440
and in the Malay peninsula,

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:11.680
which traces its roots back to Bombay.

00:36:11.680 --> 00:36:15.877
Cai luong in what's now Vietnam,
which has diverse Chinese

00:36:15.877 --> 00:36:19.794
and Western influences in
terms of music and expression,

00:36:19.794 --> 00:36:23.834
or the Komedia that we
see in the Philippines.

00:36:23.834 --> 00:36:27.370
After all, the most famous epic poem

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:30.739
of the 19th century in Tagalog literature,

00:36:30.739 --> 00:36:34.490
it is about a series of
events that take place

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:36.950
in Albania of all places.

00:36:36.950 --> 00:36:38.930
So in other words, what we see

00:36:38.930 --> 00:36:42.260
by the turn of the 20th
century in Southeast Asia

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:47.260
is the emergence of very
cosmopolitan sort of cities

00:36:47.510 --> 00:36:52.240
and towns with a cosmopolitan,
very diverse mercantile

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:55.750
and professional classes,
new forms of modern learning,

00:36:55.750 --> 00:36:59.340
knowledge, schooling, and
associational activity,

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:02.350
new forms of modernity
in terms of expression

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:06.353
and associational activity,
forms of modern consciousness.

00:37:06.353 --> 00:37:10.290
And so this template suggests this kind

00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:14.290
of teleology instead,
but leaving open a realm

00:37:14.290 --> 00:37:19.290
of indeterminacy as to when,
where, how, or whether at all

00:37:19.928 --> 00:37:24.763
it will lead to some form
of revolution making.

00:37:24.763 --> 00:37:28.300
And so throughout much of
the book, what I try and do

00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:31.200
is to hold together an
underlying framework

00:37:31.200 --> 00:37:35.796
of a kind of comparative
historical sociological analysis

00:37:35.796 --> 00:37:38.890
even while emphasizing the importance

00:37:38.890 --> 00:37:41.610
of major international conjunctures.

00:37:41.610 --> 00:37:45.936
The Spanish American
War, the Sino-Japanese

00:37:45.936 --> 00:37:49.507
and Russo-Japanese Wars, World War I

00:37:49.507 --> 00:37:51.786
and of course, World War II.

00:37:51.786 --> 00:37:55.135
But the real thrust of
the analysis in the book

00:37:55.135 --> 00:37:57.750
lies in a more structuralist kind

00:37:57.750 --> 00:38:00.320
of comparative historical sociology

00:38:00.320 --> 00:38:02.690
with an emphasis on the variance

00:38:02.690 --> 00:38:05.700
in how these different
Southeast Asian societies

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:09.450
played host to different kinds
and different constellations

00:38:09.450 --> 00:38:13.470
of cosmopolitan linkages
which provided institutional,

00:38:13.470 --> 00:38:17.020
organizational, and discursive
bases for different kinds

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:20.567
of revolutionary mobilization
as signaled in the title,

00:38:20.567 --> 00:38:23.157
"Republicanism, Communism, Islam."

00:38:24.020 --> 00:38:27.211
And there are two essential
elements in the argument

00:38:27.211 --> 00:38:31.250
along the lines of comparative
historical sociology.

00:38:31.250 --> 00:38:35.810
The first is that the early
modern era gave rise to forms

00:38:35.810 --> 00:38:38.410
of cosmopolitanism that left legacies

00:38:38.410 --> 00:38:43.410
in terms of scholasticism and
scholastic infrastructures

00:38:43.871 --> 00:38:48.871
that we see whether Catholic
or Islamic or sinographic

00:38:51.127 --> 00:38:54.010
which are important in
their varying tendencies

00:38:54.010 --> 00:38:58.220
towards absorption and
integration, into existing orders

00:38:58.220 --> 00:39:02.060
and state structures or
autonomy and independence

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:05.398
and evolution and expansion
outside of the state

00:39:05.398 --> 00:39:10.350
or evisceration by modern colonial states.

00:39:10.350 --> 00:39:13.150
And this matters in due course.

00:39:13.150 --> 00:39:16.810
And the second thing that
really matters is what happens

00:39:16.810 --> 00:39:19.040
in the late 19th and early 20th century

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:22.810
in terms of class formation,
in terms of the emergence

00:39:22.810 --> 00:39:26.598
of dominant classes and
subaltern classes that vary

00:39:26.598 --> 00:39:30.730
in different ways in terms
of their sort of capacities

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:32.920
to serve as social bases

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:35.760
for different kinds of
revolutionary projects

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:38.083
and forms of revolutionary mobilization.

00:39:39.033 --> 00:39:42.950
And I had initially
planned to then go through

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:48.450
this kind of account for
the three revolutions,

00:39:48.450 --> 00:39:52.380
but I can see that time is
short and that if I proceed

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:57.050
along those lines, I will not
leave much time for questions.

00:39:57.050 --> 00:39:59.740
So I can just give you
some choice quotes here

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:02.344
from the concluding chapter of the book

00:40:02.344 --> 00:40:06.406
that give you a flavor of
the conclusions I draw.

00:40:06.406 --> 00:40:11.406
This is a kind of a little
nugget of a concluding argument.

00:40:11.640 --> 00:40:14.257
But if we look at the
account of the Philippines,

00:40:14.257 --> 00:40:18.045
this is where I come down
in terms of emphasizing

00:40:18.045 --> 00:40:23.045
the importance of an
underlying tension and conflict

00:40:23.330 --> 00:40:28.240
between an assimilated
Chinese Mestizo dominant class

00:40:28.240 --> 00:40:32.700
that combines economic,
social, and political power

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:37.568
on a local level and runs
up against, increasingly,

00:40:37.568 --> 00:40:40.740
the entrenched powers
of the Catholic Church

00:40:40.740 --> 00:40:45.040
in the late 19th century and
has the capacity to articulate

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:50.040
and to lead forms of
mobilization in tune with

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:54.400
and in sync with similar
kinds of struggles

00:40:54.400 --> 00:40:57.630
going on in other parts
of the Catholic world

00:40:57.630 --> 00:40:58.870
in the late 19th century,

00:40:58.870 --> 00:41:03.870
the so-called culture
Wars between ultramontane,

00:41:04.030 --> 00:41:07.033
very conservative Catholicism
and various strands

00:41:07.033 --> 00:41:10.390
of republicanism and
liberalism represented

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:14.350
by Freemasonry most particularly.

00:41:14.350 --> 00:41:18.490
And that this is crucial in
terms of the infrastructure

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:22.710
for mobilization and in terms
of when and where and how

00:41:22.710 --> 00:41:27.510
and by whom we see this
kind of mobilization.

00:41:27.510 --> 00:41:30.840
It's something that that I trace up

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:34.060
into the revolution
itself, so that the figure

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:37.940
of Andres Bonifacio, a
key leader in the making

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:42.940
of the Katipunan, the
fact that he is working

00:41:42.950 --> 00:41:46.530
for a German company
in Manila's Chinatown,

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:51.530
the fact that he had served
as an actor in a local Komedia

00:41:51.980 --> 00:41:55.137
and the fact that among his
books is the famous book,

00:41:55.137 --> 00:41:58.270
"The Wandering Jew"
which depicts the Jesuits

00:41:58.270 --> 00:42:03.270
as part of this elaborate
conspiracy are all symptomatic,

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:07.118
emblematic of this broader
pattern of what made

00:42:07.118 --> 00:42:10.660
and also what limited
the Philippine Revolution

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:14.530
in terms of the limitations
of its egalitarian promise

00:42:14.530 --> 00:42:19.530
and its capacity for
organizational sort of solidity

00:42:19.730 --> 00:42:22.250
in the face of various challenges.

00:42:22.250 --> 00:42:23.840
The Katipunan within the Katipunan.

00:42:23.840 --> 00:42:28.510
And of course the massive
might of the American military

00:42:28.510 --> 00:42:31.380
as they invaded and
occupied the Philippines

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:33.810
at the turn of the 20th century.

00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:36.310
By contrast, if we look
at the Indonesian case,

00:42:36.310 --> 00:42:38.865
the emphasis is on two features

00:42:38.865 --> 00:42:42.240
of the Indonesian archipelago
that distinguish it

00:42:42.240 --> 00:42:45.420
from the Philippines and
other parts of Southeast Asia.

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:50.420
The first is the notable
autonomy or independence

00:42:51.210 --> 00:42:54.300
of various forms of Islamic schooling

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:59.064
and associational activity
which render it available

00:42:59.064 --> 00:43:04.064
for forms of mobilization
already in the early 20th century

00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:05.400
and during the Revolution.

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:10.047
And then on the other hand,
the importance and extent

00:43:10.047 --> 00:43:13.486
of large-scale plantation agriculture

00:43:13.486 --> 00:43:18.210
and railroad transport, and the creation

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:21.730
of a large scale wage labor
based industrial proletariat

00:43:21.730 --> 00:43:26.103
providing the basis for
left-wing socialist,

00:43:26.103 --> 00:43:31.103
communist labor organizing and
mobilization with communist

00:43:31.180 --> 00:43:36.060
and Islamic infrastructures
proving so crucial

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:39.180
for mobilization in the Revolusi

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:41.680
in the latter half of the 1940s

00:43:41.680 --> 00:43:46.530
as shown so brilliantly by
Kevin Fogg in his recent book.

00:43:46.530 --> 00:43:51.530
So I think that without Islam
and communism often derided

00:43:52.750 --> 00:43:56.470
by official nationalist
accounts as somehow traitors

00:43:56.470 --> 00:43:59.960
to the revolution, without
Islam and communism

00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:03.580
the Revolusi would have
had very little oomph to it

00:44:03.580 --> 00:44:04.848
in terms of the the energies

00:44:04.848 --> 00:44:07.513
that it brought to the Revolution.

00:44:08.430 --> 00:44:11.670
And yet the failure of those two

00:44:11.670 --> 00:44:15.320
kind of cosmopolitan rubrics
for mobilization to combine

00:44:15.320 --> 00:44:20.040
and fuse in a single form also
limited their effectiveness

00:44:20.040 --> 00:44:24.700
and rendered them vulnerable
to absorption, appropriation

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:26.640
and eventual evisceration

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:30.480
by a Republican state after independence.

00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:33.056
And finally, in the Vietnamese case,

00:44:33.056 --> 00:44:38.056
what I suggest is another
point of comparison,

00:44:39.840 --> 00:44:41.080
if we can get down to it.

00:44:41.080 --> 00:44:44.560
You see how much I hoped
to go through here.

00:44:44.560 --> 00:44:48.130
And the crucial thing in the
Vietnamese case is twofold.

00:44:48.130 --> 00:44:52.635
One, that the form of
sinographic cosmopolitanism

00:44:52.635 --> 00:44:57.490
that had lasted on and off for centuries

00:44:57.490 --> 00:45:02.490
in Vietnamese history die
a a death just a decade

00:45:03.860 --> 00:45:07.275
after their disappearance
in neighboring China

00:45:07.275 --> 00:45:12.275
with the forms of sinographic
learning and schooling

00:45:13.690 --> 00:45:16.830
and socialization that previously fed

00:45:16.830 --> 00:45:20.377
into an imperial state abolished

00:45:20.377 --> 00:45:25.000
as a new form of state rule emerges.

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:28.260
And so the elimination
of this kind of tradition

00:45:28.260 --> 00:45:31.885
and this kind of infrastructure
for mobilization,

00:45:31.885 --> 00:45:34.020
for socialization,
communication, and mobilization

00:45:34.020 --> 00:45:37.150
that lasted into the early
20th century in Vietnam.

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:40.610
It's elimination meant that in Vietnam,

00:45:40.610 --> 00:45:44.640
you didn't have a kind of
institutional counterweight

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:49.640
that would provide an
alternative basis to communism

00:45:51.430 --> 00:45:53.540
in the way that the Islamic schools

00:45:53.540 --> 00:45:56.520
and associations did in the Indies.

00:45:56.520 --> 00:46:01.158
And also crucially the tight integration

00:46:01.158 --> 00:46:06.158
of the Vietnamese economy into
the Southern Chinese economy

00:46:10.100 --> 00:46:14.900
really rendered the local Vietnamese,

00:46:14.900 --> 00:46:18.670
and the insistence of the
French colonial regime

00:46:18.670 --> 00:46:22.442
on segregating Chinese immigrants

00:46:22.442 --> 00:46:27.160
really rendered the local
Vietnamese business class

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:32.160
a small marginal potential
carrier for and social base

00:46:33.470 --> 00:46:38.470
for a Republican alternative
to other forms of mobilization

00:46:39.737 --> 00:46:42.620
and other kinds of egalitarian promise

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:46.420
and discourse and
revolutionary brotherhood.

00:46:46.420 --> 00:46:49.970
And this combined with the
circumstances and the geography

00:46:51.850 --> 00:46:55.080
of Vietnam within Indochina
within the French empire

00:46:55.080 --> 00:46:59.253
and of course as a neighbor
of China really disadvantaged

00:46:59.253 --> 00:47:03.290
alternatives and
challengers to what became

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:07.660
the monolithic monopoly of the Viet Minh

00:47:07.660 --> 00:47:11.053
and the Communist Party over
the revolutionary struggle.

00:47:12.310 --> 00:47:16.550
And so it's this kind
of comparative analysis

00:47:16.550 --> 00:47:21.550
combined with an attention
to international conjunctures

00:47:22.730 --> 00:47:26.120
and various forms of sympathy,
support, and solidarity

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:29.520
in different parts of the
world that provides the canvas

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:34.260
for the accounts I give
these revolutions and the way

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:39.260
in which I roll out this
initial template into an account

00:47:39.360 --> 00:47:42.630
of the trajectories and
outcomes of these revolutions,

00:47:42.630 --> 00:47:46.230
providing a kind of
denationalized, internationalized,

00:47:46.230 --> 00:47:48.240
and trans-nationalized account

00:47:48.240 --> 00:47:50.390
of revolutions in Southeast Asia.

00:47:50.390 --> 00:47:52.970
Sorry for going on for so long,

00:47:52.970 --> 00:47:57.360
but there may be many
questions about the book,

00:47:57.360 --> 00:48:01.059
and the good news is you can read the book

00:48:01.059 --> 00:48:05.915
and you can also purchase the
book at a serious discount

00:48:05.915 --> 00:48:08.450
and get all the answers there.

00:48:08.450 --> 00:48:10.373
So thank you and sorry for going over.

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:13.753
- Thank you, John.

00:48:13.753 --> 00:48:14.717
No need to apologize at all.

00:48:14.717 --> 00:48:17.100
It was a fascinating talk.

00:48:17.100 --> 00:48:18.990
I'm sure we're gonna
have a lot of questions.

00:48:18.990 --> 00:48:20.850
I'm going to stop sharing the screen

00:48:22.300 --> 00:48:24.590
just so that we can all
see each other's faces

00:48:24.590 --> 00:48:27.440
and create a little bit of
community here during the Q&amp;A.

00:48:28.310 --> 00:48:32.350
So everyone, please raise your
hand, use the hand function.

00:48:32.350 --> 00:48:34.040
And if you can't figure
out how to do that,

00:48:34.040 --> 00:48:36.880
send me a text through the chat function

00:48:36.880 --> 00:48:40.112
or wave your hand wildly,
and eventually I'll see you.

00:48:40.112 --> 00:48:43.963
But let's start with whoever
has the first question.

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:57.040
I'd also like, Kevin has a question.

00:48:57.040 --> 00:48:59.050
I was about to mention Kevin's name.

00:48:59.050 --> 00:49:03.430
I wanted to invite both
Kevin and Christian Lentz

00:49:03.430 --> 00:49:06.670
to share their book links.

00:49:06.670 --> 00:49:08.700
Out of modesty, they probably
won't want to do that,

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:10.590
but they were both called
out in the lecture.

00:49:10.590 --> 00:49:13.260
So I give them permission
to share their links

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:14.800
to their books, to the rest of us

00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:15.633
'cause I'm sure a lot of people-

00:49:15.633 --> 00:49:17.263
- I can pull copies off my shelves.

00:49:18.190 --> 00:49:20.110
- So we'll start with
Kevin, and then the question

00:49:20.110 --> 00:49:22.900
from Christian right
after that, and then...

00:49:22.900 --> 00:49:24.700
- Well, that's very kind of you, Erik.

00:49:24.700 --> 00:49:25.670
And thank you, John, so much

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:28.101
for a really interesting engagement.

00:49:28.101 --> 00:49:30.960
The question that I
wondered, and I'm not sure

00:49:30.960 --> 00:49:33.510
that the book goes this far is about some

00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:36.570
of the near misses in Southeast Asia.

00:49:36.570 --> 00:49:39.020
You, I don't want to say derided,

00:49:39.020 --> 00:49:42.060
but you quoted Lee Kuan Yew
to deride the Malaysian cases,

00:49:42.060 --> 00:49:44.230
getting handed independence
on a silver platter,

00:49:44.230 --> 00:49:48.020
but they did have a quote
unquote emergency for 10 years.

00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:50.800
They did have folks who wanted
a revolutionary direction.

00:49:50.800 --> 00:49:52.670
You know, there are other
countries in Southeast Asia

00:49:52.670 --> 00:49:56.100
where this could have happened,
and there are also ways

00:49:56.100 --> 00:49:58.260
in which the countries
that we wound up with.

00:49:58.260 --> 00:50:00.420
I mean, Indonesia might not
have wound up as Indonesia.

00:50:00.420 --> 00:50:03.285
It could have wound up as
two things or as five things.

00:50:03.285 --> 00:50:08.120
I, as a historian, there's
some worry when we look

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:09.900
at this big structural
thing that you've accepted

00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:11.620
the structures that came after

00:50:11.620 --> 00:50:12.670
and then gone back to explain them

00:50:12.670 --> 00:50:14.868
instead of wondering what
could have happened otherwise.

00:50:14.868 --> 00:50:17.050
Do you talk at all about the near misses

00:50:17.050 --> 00:50:18.537
or the contingencies in this

00:50:18.537 --> 00:50:21.313
and where that might have gone for others?

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:28.730
- Should I go ahead and answer
individual questions, Erik?

00:50:28.730 --> 00:50:29.920
- Yeah, please do.

00:50:29.920 --> 00:50:31.470
- Okay, it's a great question.

00:50:31.470 --> 00:50:35.204
I mean, they're in a way,
two elements of an answer.

00:50:35.204 --> 00:50:40.204
One is that I do extend very
briefly in the conclusion

00:50:40.434 --> 00:50:44.650
the analysis to cover other
countries in Southeast Asia.

00:50:44.650 --> 00:50:47.220
So I take Indonesia and
then look at Malaysia

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:49.050
and say, well, yes, on the one hand,

00:50:49.050 --> 00:50:52.450
we see a very impressive form

00:50:52.450 --> 00:50:54.920
of Malayan Communist Party mobilization

00:50:54.920 --> 00:50:56.990
and insurrectionary activity that lasts

00:50:57.881 --> 00:51:00.696
from the late '40 into the 1950s,

00:51:00.696 --> 00:51:04.600
but it's limited in considerable measure.

00:51:04.600 --> 00:51:07.710
Revisionist historians question
this, but in large measure

00:51:07.710 --> 00:51:12.160
to the Chinese population
and to certain segments

00:51:12.160 --> 00:51:14.677
of the population in
terms of social class.

00:51:14.677 --> 00:51:18.660
And I think that a
contrast in terms of Islam

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:21.200
is also very strong that you can see

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:24.023
that in contrast with
Indonesia where you have,

00:51:25.001 --> 00:51:27.794
basically Islam is left outside

00:51:27.794 --> 00:51:30.340
the control of the colonial state.

00:51:30.340 --> 00:51:33.220
It's not controlled by
the local aristocracy.

00:51:33.220 --> 00:51:35.190
That's very different under
British colonial rule.

00:51:35.190 --> 00:51:37.404
And you have nothing like
the kind of infrastructure

00:51:37.404 --> 00:51:41.047
of autonomous, independent,
mobilizational capacity.

00:51:41.932 --> 00:51:46.800
And you have also a very
different form of class formation.

00:51:46.800 --> 00:51:48.280
The Javanese peasantry compared

00:51:48.280 --> 00:51:50.820
to the Malay peasantry, very different.

00:51:50.820 --> 00:51:55.820
So there is that, I do play
out that structuralist analysis

00:51:55.909 --> 00:51:57.460
in terms of the comparison.

00:51:57.460 --> 00:52:02.133
Now to get to your, you're
not a proper historian,

00:52:02.133 --> 00:52:04.920
you're just one of these
damn political scientists

00:52:04.920 --> 00:52:08.570
who are retrospectively
cherry picking the history

00:52:08.570 --> 00:52:11.420
and avoiding the kind

00:52:11.420 --> 00:52:15.570
of alternative moments and conjunctures.

00:52:15.570 --> 00:52:19.180
So I do talk about the Sarekat Islam,

00:52:19.180 --> 00:52:22.130
and there is new scholarship
coming out about that

00:52:22.130 --> 00:52:24.390
and about that period.

00:52:24.390 --> 00:52:27.539
And you yourself are working
on these possibilities

00:52:27.539 --> 00:52:31.510
for sort of combining Islam and communism.

00:52:31.510 --> 00:52:36.510
And I do dwell quite
a bit on that and how,

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:39.950
if not fully, why it doesn't happen.

00:52:39.950 --> 00:52:41.764
Like the nature and extent to which

00:52:41.764 --> 00:52:45.390
it begins to come together
and then it unravels.

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:48.660
And it happens across the 20th century

00:52:48.660 --> 00:52:50.730
in many different contexts.

00:52:50.730 --> 00:52:54.020
And, you know, if Ali
Shariati had not died

00:52:54.020 --> 00:52:55.921
just before the Iranian revolution

00:52:55.921 --> 00:52:58.372
would things have gone differently?

00:52:58.372 --> 00:53:02.210
We don't have, you know, it
seems to be a broader pattern.

00:53:02.210 --> 00:53:07.210
So I kind of end up at least
giving some time and space

00:53:08.160 --> 00:53:13.160
to the woulda coulda shouldas
if not at least dwell on them.

00:53:18.500 --> 00:53:19.333
- Thank you.

00:53:21.940 --> 00:53:23.190
- Erik, should I go ahead?

00:53:23.190 --> 00:53:25.010
- Yeah, thanks, John, I was
just going to ask Christian

00:53:25.010 --> 00:53:27.114
to ask his question, please do.

00:53:27.114 --> 00:53:29.010
- Well first let me just say, I think

00:53:29.010 --> 00:53:30.610
as I just said to Eric in a chat

00:53:30.610 --> 00:53:34.440
I'm still blushing, John, for
your nice call out of my book,

00:53:34.440 --> 00:53:37.480
and it's very kind of you to mention that.

00:53:37.480 --> 00:53:40.160
I'm also quite honored to be
mentioned in the same breath

00:53:40.160 --> 00:53:43.670
as Kevin Fogg, my colleague
here at North Carolina.

00:53:43.670 --> 00:53:47.464
So I had sort of one comment
perhaps and then one question.

00:53:47.464 --> 00:53:50.610
My comment is, I think in
some ways it seems to me,

00:53:50.610 --> 00:53:54.690
I just happened to finish
"Under Three Flags" yesterday,

00:53:54.690 --> 00:53:57.747
Ben Anderson's final monograph
about the sort of anarchism

00:53:57.747 --> 00:54:00.540
and its role in Southeast Asian history.

00:54:00.540 --> 00:54:02.080
And so it seems to me
what you're doing then

00:54:02.080 --> 00:54:04.750
is you're sort of bringing
this sort of late Anderson

00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:06.710
into dialogue with early Anderson

00:54:06.710 --> 00:54:08.810
and his work in "Imagined
Communities" to try and,

00:54:08.810 --> 00:54:12.120
it was a work of synthesis
then rather than a rejection

00:54:12.120 --> 00:54:14.630
of that earlier sort of, as you called it,

00:54:14.630 --> 00:54:16.780
template for national consciousness.

00:54:16.780 --> 00:54:18.280
That's I guess my comment.

00:54:18.280 --> 00:54:21.640
And then my question is, all
of us who have gone to Yale

00:54:21.640 --> 00:54:24.820
and Cornell again, I'm just
referring to the shout out

00:54:24.820 --> 00:54:28.505
from Erik before, are
well aware that our field

00:54:28.505 --> 00:54:30.990
of Southeast Asian studies came of age

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:32.910
in the United States during the Cold war.

00:54:32.910 --> 00:54:35.400
And so I think there is,
as you suggest, an argument

00:54:35.400 --> 00:54:38.990
for the kind of nationalist reading

00:54:38.990 --> 00:54:41.960
of Southeast Asian history
as a way to counter

00:54:41.960 --> 00:54:44.720
the American Imperial
project to put it quickly.

00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:47.970
So given that we are now in
this different moment, right,

00:54:47.970 --> 00:54:50.030
what do you think are
the essential ingredients

00:54:50.030 --> 00:54:53.690
for this revisiting of
Southeast Asian studies?

00:54:53.690 --> 00:54:58.690
Are we all now under the,
sort of the lure, if you will,

00:54:59.170 --> 00:55:01.830
of globalization to try and
think about Southeast Asia

00:55:01.830 --> 00:55:02.730
in a more decentered way?

00:55:02.730 --> 00:55:05.330
What do you think are this sort
of fundaments, if you will,

00:55:05.330 --> 00:55:06.730
of the intellectual situation

00:55:06.730 --> 00:55:09.350
that are contributing to this revision?

00:55:09.350 --> 00:55:10.183
Thank you.

00:55:11.740 --> 00:55:13.660
- Great questions.

00:55:13.660 --> 00:55:18.015
In terms of Ben Anderson's
"Under Three Flags,"

00:55:18.015 --> 00:55:22.450
I mean, I've been working on this project

00:55:22.450 --> 00:55:27.346
since, embarrassingly since 2003.

00:55:27.346 --> 00:55:31.790
And he was aware of what I was
doing and I think, you know,

00:55:31.790 --> 00:55:32.980
and he was supportive of it.

00:55:32.980 --> 00:55:36.544
So it was not written, the
book is dedicated to him

00:55:36.544 --> 00:55:38.800
and it is not written against him.

00:55:38.800 --> 00:55:41.040
And I actually, there's a great quote

00:55:41.040 --> 00:55:44.560
from "Under Three Flags"
that I included in the book

00:55:44.560 --> 00:55:48.567
because the readers, the
reviewers of the manuscript said,

00:55:48.567 --> 00:55:50.730
"You know, there's, we're not completely."

00:55:50.730 --> 00:55:51.743
One of them at least said,

00:55:51.743 --> 00:55:53.670
"I'm not fully comfortable with the way

00:55:53.670 --> 00:55:57.310
in which you're engaging
with and framing this book."

00:55:57.310 --> 00:55:59.210
And there was a bit of concern about that.

00:55:59.210 --> 00:56:03.490
So I think I make clear
that I'm, you know,

00:56:03.490 --> 00:56:07.340
that it's the template
rather than the book.

00:56:07.340 --> 00:56:11.330
And that to go back to
Kevin's question of,

00:56:11.330 --> 00:56:13.995
well, could Indonesia
have looked different?

00:56:13.995 --> 00:56:17.260
Those sorts of issues, I
think that there's a kind

00:56:17.260 --> 00:56:20.690
of impressive longevity
to imagine communities

00:56:20.690 --> 00:56:25.290
in terms of almost an implicit
or explicit prediction

00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:27.830
that these nations will stay as such

00:56:27.830 --> 00:56:29.010
in terms of their boundaries.

00:56:29.010 --> 00:56:31.290
And so I think, you know,
if you're interested

00:56:31.290 --> 00:56:34.890
in those sorts of questions,
with the interesting rickles

00:56:34.890 --> 00:56:38.110
which Chris Gosha offers
in the case of Indochina,

00:56:38.110 --> 00:56:42.380
you know, I would say,
I fully endorse that.

00:56:42.380 --> 00:56:44.160
It is the template.

00:56:44.160 --> 00:56:47.796
And so to get to your final question,

00:56:47.796 --> 00:56:52.796
I think if we are going to not
be so concerned with saying,

00:56:56.780 --> 00:57:00.050
oh, Southeast Asians are
ineffably Southeast Asian

00:57:00.050 --> 00:57:02.990
in some way or Southeast Asians

00:57:02.990 --> 00:57:07.370
are really deeply
nationalists in some way,

00:57:07.370 --> 00:57:11.260
but instead we're gonna
do kind of global history

00:57:11.260 --> 00:57:13.900
or world history then it's
important to make sure

00:57:13.900 --> 00:57:17.560
that Southeast Asians are
seen as actors in this.

00:57:17.560 --> 00:57:20.680
And there's a wonderful PhD thesis

00:57:20.680 --> 00:57:24.080
from the University of Hawaii
which shows how it's not only

00:57:24.080 --> 00:57:27.870
as scholars tell us that the
first globalization began

00:57:27.870 --> 00:57:32.360
in Manila with the
connection of Asian, American

00:57:32.360 --> 00:57:35.340
and European trade made
possible through the galleon,

00:57:35.340 --> 00:57:40.170
but it's that Filipino
shipbuilders, Filipino loggers,

00:57:40.170 --> 00:57:42.630
Filipino workers who
repaired and built ships

00:57:42.630 --> 00:57:45.900
and then Filipino sailors who
navigated these ships back

00:57:45.900 --> 00:57:48.834
across the Pacific, they
played a crucial role.

00:57:48.834 --> 00:57:51.767
And so it's not just that
Manila was like a kind of,

00:57:51.767 --> 00:57:53.840
you know, a site for this

00:57:53.840 --> 00:57:57.230
but that Filipinos were
active, crucial participants.

00:57:57.230 --> 00:57:59.384
It didn't just happen in Southeast Asia.

00:57:59.384 --> 00:58:03.840
And so, you know, I think
it's crucial for the accounts

00:58:05.555 --> 00:58:10.555
to show the active creative
roles of Southeast Asians

00:58:10.640 --> 00:58:14.523
making the most if not always
the best of globalizations.

00:58:15.610 --> 00:58:19.170
And I guess in political terms,
for me that maybe something

00:58:19.170 --> 00:58:22.320
that somehow still stays with me is in,

00:58:22.320 --> 00:58:25.610
I don't know, was it 2010,
the election here in the UK,

00:58:25.610 --> 00:58:29.760
all three very conventional
and conservative politicians

00:58:29.760 --> 00:58:32.110
when asked, who were
running for prime minister

00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:33.836
when asked who they
admired most in the world

00:58:33.836 --> 00:58:35.750
they also said, oh, Nelson Mandela,

00:58:35.750 --> 00:58:37.790
Nelson Mandela, Nelson Mandela.

00:58:37.790 --> 00:58:42.141
And so Nelson Mandela is cute and cuddly

00:58:42.141 --> 00:58:47.141
as a kind of hero struggling
for South African nationalism

00:58:48.527 --> 00:58:53.527
as it were, but the fact
that he was supported by

00:58:54.420 --> 00:58:58.386
if not a member of the South
African Communist Party,

00:58:58.386 --> 00:59:00.790
that rendered him untouchable.

00:59:00.790 --> 00:59:03.610
And no one would shake
his hand or ask for him

00:59:03.610 --> 00:59:06.940
to get out of jail all those
long years in the 20th century.

00:59:06.940 --> 00:59:11.940
So it's quite hypocritical
and a convenient forgetfulness

00:59:13.510 --> 00:59:17.171
for us to forget the role
of the the communists

00:59:17.171 --> 00:59:21.370
and the Islamists, or the
role of communism and Islam

00:59:21.370 --> 00:59:23.680
in the making of the
Indonesian Revolution,

00:59:23.680 --> 00:59:25.940
of all sorts of inconvenient people

00:59:25.940 --> 00:59:29.563
in the making of the Vietnamese
Revolution as you show us.

00:59:31.690 --> 00:59:35.420
So I think to denationalize can be helpful

00:59:35.420 --> 00:59:39.420
because I think we've seen a
great deal of renationalisation

00:59:39.420 --> 00:59:42.160
and recentralization of
power in different parts

00:59:42.160 --> 00:59:46.690
of the world in the past 10, 20 years.

00:59:46.690 --> 00:59:48.650
In Russia, in China, in Turkey,

00:59:48.650 --> 00:59:50.280
in India, in the United States.

00:59:50.280 --> 00:59:54.510
Like, why not continue
to work to denationalize?

00:59:57.890 --> 00:59:58.723
Yeah.

01:00:00.260 --> 01:00:02.920
- John, I'm gonna jump in with a question.

01:00:02.920 --> 01:00:04.500
I see that Kelvin has a question,

01:00:04.500 --> 01:00:06.670
but I didn't see the hand until right now.

01:00:06.670 --> 01:00:09.020
And I've already started
asking my question technically

01:00:09.020 --> 01:00:13.673
so I'll forge ahead and then
Kelvin will be right after.

01:00:14.700 --> 01:00:16.907
I wanted to ask you a
little bit about this term

01:00:16.907 --> 01:00:21.060
that you used, scholasticism,
exactly what you mean by that.

01:00:21.060 --> 01:00:23.500
And it may be a term of art that I just,

01:00:23.500 --> 01:00:26.181
I'm not up on the language.

01:00:26.181 --> 01:00:29.051
And how you call that a certain kind

01:00:29.051 --> 01:00:31.763
of cosmopolitan connection,

01:00:32.750 --> 01:00:34.930
and how you might differentiate that

01:00:34.930 --> 01:00:38.730
from Ben Anderson's famous
points about print capitalism

01:00:38.730 --> 01:00:40.610
because it seems that it's also

01:00:40.610 --> 01:00:43.310
a kind of cosmopolitan connection.

01:00:43.310 --> 01:00:46.429
And I'm wondering, are you arguing

01:00:46.429 --> 01:00:49.810
against the formative
role of print capitalism?

01:00:49.810 --> 01:00:53.380
Or are you trying to say
that there are other forms

01:00:53.380 --> 01:00:57.700
of cosmopolitan connections
that are sort of,

01:00:57.700 --> 01:01:00.550
we ignore them when we
pay too much attention

01:01:00.550 --> 01:01:03.063
to a certain kind of cosmopolitanism?

01:01:04.070 --> 01:01:06.700
And I also wanted to use
this as an opportunity

01:01:06.700 --> 01:01:10.160
to make a mention of next week's Brown Bag

01:01:10.160 --> 01:01:14.560
because Kathleen Gutierrez's
work on botany is actually,

01:01:14.560 --> 01:01:17.700
it sounds to me like it's
actually unexpectedly

01:01:17.700 --> 01:01:20.220
doing the same kind of thing
that you're talking about

01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:24.220
because she talks about how
through the rise of botany

01:01:24.220 --> 01:01:26.610
in the Philippines there's a kind

01:01:26.610 --> 01:01:29.830
of botanical cosmopolitanism that emerges.

01:01:29.830 --> 01:01:33.156
And there was a concept of
Southeast Asia that she describes

01:01:33.156 --> 01:01:36.940
called Malesia which was a floristic zone,

01:01:36.940 --> 01:01:40.720
like a floristic region among
the scientific community.

01:01:40.720 --> 01:01:41.770
And so I think there's a kind

01:01:41.770 --> 01:01:45.740
of unexpected convergence
there, and also some other,

01:01:45.740 --> 01:01:47.760
the scholarship that recently been out,

01:01:47.760 --> 01:01:50.710
in Claire Eddington's work
on colonial psychiatry

01:01:50.710 --> 01:01:53.070
talks a lot about how the psychiatrists

01:01:53.070 --> 01:01:56.640
in colonial Indochine were looking

01:01:56.640 --> 01:01:59.900
to the Dutch psychiatrists to get ideas

01:01:59.900 --> 01:02:03.950
about how to create new asylums
and those kinds of things.

01:02:03.950 --> 01:02:07.686
So there's an interesting
emergence of the scholarship

01:02:07.686 --> 01:02:09.928
that seems to be doing what you're doing

01:02:09.928 --> 01:02:12.350
but without the kind of nice framework

01:02:12.350 --> 01:02:13.710
that you're giving it to.

01:02:13.710 --> 01:02:16.340
And so I wanted to signal
that, remind everyone to come

01:02:16.340 --> 01:02:20.140
to next week's Brown Bag and
also just ask you to clarify

01:02:20.140 --> 01:02:23.160
the difference between the scholasticism

01:02:23.160 --> 01:02:25.530
and your form of cosmopolitan connection

01:02:25.530 --> 01:02:28.163
and that famous idea of print capitalism.

01:02:30.192 --> 01:02:31.564
- Okay, great question.

01:02:31.564 --> 01:02:34.840
So if you look at "Imagined
Communities" you'll see

01:02:34.840 --> 01:02:38.770
that Ben Anderson talks about
kind of pre-national forms

01:02:38.770 --> 01:02:42.380
of cosmopolitan consciousness
that he associates

01:02:42.380 --> 01:02:46.610
with sacred languages that one might link

01:02:46.610 --> 01:02:51.610
to very broad transoceanic
trends, continental

01:02:53.581 --> 01:02:57.300
kind of religious communities,
whether it's Latin

01:02:57.300 --> 01:03:02.300
or Pali or Arabic, classical
Arabic, or classical Chinese.

01:03:03.040 --> 01:03:08.040
And I guess what I see as
significant is much more specific,

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:13.880
namely the institutions of
scholarly or scholastic activity

01:03:16.130 --> 01:03:19.550
that we see in terms of seminaries,

01:03:19.550 --> 01:03:23.680
in terms of what turn into
the madrassas or the Pesantren

01:03:25.500 --> 01:03:30.500
over centuries and their
counterparts in Vietnam

01:03:30.620 --> 01:03:33.740
that are analogous and interrelated

01:03:33.740 --> 01:03:37.197
to what's happening in
China in terms of every year

01:03:37.197 --> 01:03:40.900
and these patterns of
circulation and connectedness

01:03:40.900 --> 01:03:43.950
that are sociologically quite
interesting and important

01:03:43.950 --> 01:03:46.970
because, you know, if in
village schools there are people

01:03:46.970 --> 01:03:51.010
studying "The Four
Books and Five Classics"

01:03:51.010 --> 01:03:52.441
and then they go to the town

01:03:52.441 --> 01:03:54.540
and then they go to the bigger market town

01:03:54.540 --> 01:03:56.690
and then they go to the provincial capital

01:03:56.690 --> 01:04:01.690
and eventually they go to Hui
for the exams, that is a form

01:04:04.650 --> 01:04:08.439
of connectedness over
decades and centuries

01:04:08.439 --> 01:04:12.433
that you then would see
with Islamic counterparts

01:04:12.433 --> 01:04:17.160
or in terms of Catholic
schools and seminaries.

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:19.994
And that's very important, I think.

01:04:19.994 --> 01:04:23.413
It is notably almost all male.

01:04:24.770 --> 01:04:27.810
So it's similar to Anderson's emphasis

01:04:27.810 --> 01:04:31.483
on kind of school boy
nationalism in a way.

01:04:32.620 --> 01:04:37.620
But I think that these kinds
of connectedness, they,

01:04:39.770 --> 01:04:43.000
regardless of the content,
they have different forms

01:04:43.000 --> 01:04:44.120
and they have different histories

01:04:44.120 --> 01:04:45.770
in terms of what happens to them.

01:04:46.990 --> 01:04:51.570
They evolve and attach
themselves into states.

01:04:51.570 --> 01:04:53.990
And then they die if the
state dispenses with them

01:04:53.990 --> 01:04:56.316
as we see in Vietnam.

01:04:56.316 --> 01:04:59.800
In the case of Islam,
they evolve and evolve

01:04:59.800 --> 01:05:02.120
and survive outside of the state.

01:05:02.120 --> 01:05:04.360
And we see in the case of Catholicism,

01:05:04.360 --> 01:05:07.911
that they evolve in diverse
and conflicted directions.

01:05:07.911 --> 01:05:12.710
So I think that it's important
to trace those histories

01:05:12.710 --> 01:05:17.190
because I think that they vary
in ways that are important

01:05:17.190 --> 01:05:20.743
for these revolutions
and for these societies.

01:05:20.743 --> 01:05:23.330
So that's what I mean by these sorts of,

01:05:23.330 --> 01:05:26.820
so they're pre-modern
forms of cosmopolitanism

01:05:26.820 --> 01:05:31.820
and scholasticism that
then evolve with modernity

01:05:32.510 --> 01:05:36.300
and with their new experiments,
with Islamic modernism,

01:05:36.300 --> 01:05:40.490
Islamic reformism, that
even the Dominicans decide

01:05:40.490 --> 01:05:41.870
they have to teach medicine

01:05:41.870 --> 01:05:44.750
and science in the late 19th century

01:05:44.750 --> 01:05:48.550
which the Jesuits are
doing decades earlier.

01:05:48.550 --> 01:05:50.310
So I think that's what I mean.

01:05:50.310 --> 01:05:52.697
And that's what I emphasize in the book.

01:05:52.697 --> 01:05:55.510
So it's a bit of that kind of,

01:05:55.510 --> 01:05:59.483
it's a bit like the Southeast
Asian studies. (laughs)

01:06:00.970 --> 01:06:01.803
- That's great.

01:06:01.803 --> 01:06:04.640
I actually think that if you looked more

01:06:04.640 --> 01:06:06.480
into Vietnamese Catholicism, too

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:09.200
although the numbers are not
the same for the Philippines

01:06:09.200 --> 01:06:10.870
so they don't create the possibility

01:06:10.870 --> 01:06:14.330
of total revolutionary transformation,

01:06:14.330 --> 01:06:17.142
but some of the same
dynamics you're describing

01:06:17.142 --> 01:06:20.160
also take place in Vietnamese Catholicism

01:06:20.160 --> 01:06:25.160
and the kind of circulation
of a cosmopolitan network.

01:06:25.800 --> 01:06:29.170
There's actually even a
secondary layer of almost

01:06:29.170 --> 01:06:32.550
an alternative state system
with different counties,

01:06:32.550 --> 01:06:37.550
like these parish-style
counties that totally give you

01:06:38.430 --> 01:06:40.700
a different geography of what is known

01:06:40.700 --> 01:06:43.261
as the nation of Vietnam.

01:06:43.261 --> 01:06:45.470
But I'll let Kevin ask his question.

01:06:45.470 --> 01:06:48.470
After Kevin we have a
question from Jennifer Gaynor.

01:06:48.470 --> 01:06:52.297
And then after Jennifer, we'll
turn to Rianne Subijanto.

01:06:54.870 --> 01:06:55.930
- Hi, Professor Sidel.

01:06:55.930 --> 01:06:57.500
Thank you so much for your presentation

01:06:57.500 --> 01:06:58.883
as well as for your book.

01:07:00.050 --> 01:07:01.820
I thought it was really interesting.

01:07:01.820 --> 01:07:03.870
And as someone working broadly on this

01:07:03.870 --> 01:07:07.020
into a movement of various
internationalist projects,

01:07:07.020 --> 01:07:09.460
I found just the ambition of your book

01:07:09.460 --> 01:07:11.663
to be incredibly admirable.

01:07:13.290 --> 01:07:15.380
I have a question which
is which really builds

01:07:15.380 --> 01:07:16.980
on Professor Harms's question

01:07:16.980 --> 01:07:20.620
on scholasticism and cosmopolitanism.

01:07:20.620 --> 01:07:22.130
And I guess I'm really curious

01:07:22.130 --> 01:07:25.360
about your use of the term cosmopolitanism

01:07:25.360 --> 01:07:26.976
in relation to other terms

01:07:26.976 --> 01:07:30.343
that may be in circulation
during this period.

01:07:30.343 --> 01:07:32.840
So if you look at the
Kompeten and if you look

01:07:32.840 --> 01:07:36.430
at the Baku Conference, I
believe that a term that most

01:07:36.430 --> 01:07:41.155
of this cohort of Marxists was
using was internationalism.

01:07:41.155 --> 01:07:45.470
And if you look the different
parts of Islam as modernism

01:07:45.470 --> 01:07:47.650
and of pan-Islamism on the sub-continent

01:07:47.650 --> 01:07:51.970
and in Southeast Asia,
the vision of universalism

01:07:51.970 --> 01:07:54.720
that it posited was one of the Ummah,

01:07:54.720 --> 01:07:56.460
as they took the Ummah as a sort

01:07:56.460 --> 01:07:58.844
of spatial scale of reference.

01:07:58.844 --> 01:08:03.844
And I guess the term
cosmopolitanism, as you mentioned,

01:08:04.217 --> 01:08:08.040
in a different sort of
conceptualization seems

01:08:08.040 --> 01:08:10.363
to be very linguistically bound.

01:08:10.363 --> 01:08:11.900
So you have the Sanskrit cosmopolis.

01:08:11.900 --> 01:08:13.230
You have the Arabic cosmopolis.

01:08:13.230 --> 01:08:16.243
You have the Persian cosmopolis.

01:08:17.533 --> 01:08:19.450
And I was wondering if you
could elaborate a bit more

01:08:19.450 --> 01:08:22.070
about perhaps the
differences and similarities,

01:08:22.070 --> 01:08:23.600
where they converge
and where they diverge,

01:08:23.600 --> 01:08:28.160
these different scales of
thinking, theorizing universalism.

01:08:28.160 --> 01:08:30.630
How is internationalism
perhaps a different

01:08:30.630 --> 01:08:34.770
or similar project compared
to cosmopolitanism?

01:08:34.770 --> 01:08:36.830
Analytically, what do
they open up in terms

01:08:36.830 --> 01:08:39.363
of how we think about these projects?

01:08:40.388 --> 01:08:44.800
And how would we think
about new role of language

01:08:44.800 --> 01:08:49.257
in perhaps forging these new
links and in this late 19th,

01:08:49.257 --> 01:08:51.930
early 20th century moment,
keeping in mind, for instance

01:08:51.930 --> 01:08:55.650
what Ben Anderson in "Under
Three Flags" would describe

01:08:55.650 --> 01:08:59.203
as the true internationalism
of the polyglot, right.

01:09:00.560 --> 01:09:02.320
And I guess a related question to that

01:09:02.320 --> 01:09:04.470
centers around this
really fascinating figure

01:09:04.470 --> 01:09:09.440
in your first chapter of
Mirsaid Sultan-Galiev,

01:09:09.440 --> 01:09:12.010
him being the so-called architect

01:09:12.010 --> 01:09:14.710
of Muslim quote unquote national communism

01:09:14.710 --> 01:09:17.530
in the Soviet union, in
his sort of resolution

01:09:17.530 --> 01:09:22.160
of the Tatar Kirgisia
Central Asian question.

01:09:22.160 --> 01:09:27.160
I was wondering to what extent
was, if Sultan-Galiev himself

01:09:27.520 --> 01:09:32.280
attempted to think about the
minoritized status of Muslims

01:09:32.280 --> 01:09:35.380
in the Soviet Union through the
frame of national communism,

01:09:35.380 --> 01:09:38.130
he's implicitly positing
a sort of congruence

01:09:38.130 --> 01:09:41.010
between the project of
internationalist communism

01:09:41.010 --> 01:09:42.950
with one of nationalism on the other.

01:09:42.950 --> 01:09:45.450
And so I guess to what
extent would that apply

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:49.080
to Southeast Asia where
nationalism and internationalism

01:09:49.080 --> 01:09:51.023
rather than being opposed to one another

01:09:51.023 --> 01:09:55.181
might actually be consonant
or congruent with each other

01:09:55.181 --> 01:09:59.290
in the sort of Sultan-Galiev sense?

01:09:59.290 --> 01:10:01.790
But thank you so much again
for your presentation.

01:10:04.500 --> 01:10:07.523
- That's the most, that's
like the toughest question

01:10:07.523 --> 01:10:09.803
anyone could could ever ask.

01:10:10.990 --> 01:10:12.380
It's a really great question.

01:10:12.380 --> 01:10:16.030
And I think my knowledge and skills

01:10:16.030 --> 01:10:20.910
and theoretical sophistication
really constrained me

01:10:20.910 --> 01:10:25.910
to really undertake a
kind of ambitious project

01:10:25.950 --> 01:10:29.670
to kind of compare
cosmopolitanism, so called.

01:10:29.670 --> 01:10:32.840
But I think you're absolutely
right in your suggestion

01:10:32.840 --> 01:10:35.250
that they must vary considerably

01:10:35.250 --> 01:10:40.020
in terms of what they really
consist of and comprise.

01:10:40.020 --> 01:10:45.020
And I think most clearly
like in your comment

01:10:46.350 --> 01:10:51.130
about Sultan-Galiev and of
communist internationalism

01:10:51.130 --> 01:10:54.160
or Muslim national communism,

01:10:54.160 --> 01:10:56.370
the nation state is part of it.

01:10:56.370 --> 01:10:58.140
You know, in Sheldon Pollock's

01:10:58.140 --> 01:11:01.670
or other versions of
the Sanskrit cosmopolis,

01:11:01.670 --> 01:11:04.880
that there's a great deal of
attention to court culture

01:11:04.880 --> 01:11:06.470
and court literature and so forth.

01:11:06.470 --> 01:11:08.580
But there isn't a conception of anything

01:11:08.580 --> 01:11:11.610
resembling like a broader community.

01:11:11.610 --> 01:11:15.600
And that might also true
in forms of sinographic

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:18.594
so-called cosmopolitanism
where there's a conception

01:11:18.594 --> 01:11:22.280
of civilized rather
than barbarian behavior

01:11:22.280 --> 01:11:26.729
and of forms of propriety
and refinement and knowledge

01:11:26.729 --> 01:11:29.690
that don't embrace a broader community

01:11:29.690 --> 01:11:33.460
in the way that an Ummah might do.

01:11:33.460 --> 01:11:36.414
So I think there is huge variance

01:11:36.414 --> 01:11:41.350
in how these so-called
cosmopolitanisms operate.

01:11:41.350 --> 01:11:44.490
And I'm painfully aware that
among political theorists

01:11:44.490 --> 01:11:48.220
and scholars of international relations

01:11:48.220 --> 01:11:52.330
and other disciplines that
the term cosmopolitanism

01:11:52.330 --> 01:11:55.180
is used in many different ways.

01:11:55.180 --> 01:11:57.790
And for me it's kind of faute de mieux

01:11:57.790 --> 01:12:01.236
that I'm using it given the alternatives.

01:12:01.236 --> 01:12:06.236
To use the term transnationalism
clearly doesn't suffice.

01:12:06.960 --> 01:12:11.510
And I think it's worth
nodding back in the direction

01:12:11.510 --> 01:12:15.725
of "Imagined Communities"
where Anderson himself

01:12:15.725 --> 01:12:20.725
uses the term and to
begin with some attention

01:12:20.930 --> 01:12:24.490
to the legacies of those pre-national

01:12:24.490 --> 01:12:26.410
forms of connectedness.

01:12:26.410 --> 01:12:31.410
So, you know, the book will
be disappointing to readers

01:12:33.340 --> 01:12:38.340
with great theoretical
sophistication, I think.

01:12:40.676 --> 01:12:42.930
- Far from it, it's not
disappointing at all.

01:12:42.930 --> 01:12:44.890
I greatly, greatly enjoyed it.

01:12:44.890 --> 01:12:46.203
- Okay, well, many thanks.

01:12:47.110 --> 01:12:48.680
And thanks for the great question.

01:12:48.680 --> 01:12:51.190
And you know, I'm glad
that there are other people

01:12:51.190 --> 01:12:54.270
interested in Sultan-Galiev
because to go back

01:12:54.270 --> 01:12:58.000
to Kevin Fogg's question, I think he's one

01:12:58.000 --> 01:13:02.006
of these interesting kind
of also-rans of history

01:13:02.006 --> 01:13:07.006
sort of like Tan Malaka in
terms of what he represented

01:13:07.560 --> 01:13:11.763
in terms of possibilities,
even working under Stalin.

01:13:15.660 --> 01:13:17.080
- Thank you.

01:13:17.080 --> 01:13:18.860
Jennifer Gaynor, you had a question.

01:13:18.860 --> 01:13:22.383
And then after Jennifer, we
will have Rianne Subijanto.

01:13:24.640 --> 01:13:26.480
- First I just want to
say, thank you, John

01:13:26.480 --> 01:13:29.290
for a wonderful talk, and I
can't wait to read the book.

01:13:29.290 --> 01:13:31.670
So take anything I have to
say with a grain of salt

01:13:31.670 --> 01:13:33.340
'cause I haven't read the book yet,

01:13:33.340 --> 01:13:35.430
but I'm really looking forward to it.

01:13:35.430 --> 01:13:36.830
And I'm sort of surprised

01:13:36.830 --> 01:13:39.390
that you haven't mentioned
Takashi Shiraishi

01:13:39.390 --> 01:13:41.650
in all this sort of influences on you

01:13:41.650 --> 01:13:43.263
because his "Age in Motion"

01:13:44.100 --> 01:13:46.300
feels like it's been a big influence

01:13:46.300 --> 01:13:49.010
in the conceptualization of the project.

01:13:49.010 --> 01:13:52.310
I don't know if that's true, but I mean,

01:13:52.310 --> 01:13:54.960
who else could have brought
in not just Blumentritt

01:13:54.960 --> 01:13:58.032
but taken us over to
Zinoviev and then Dorochevsky

01:13:58.032 --> 01:13:58.865
and not just Guangzhou.

01:13:58.865 --> 01:14:00.330
And I love the way,

01:14:00.330 --> 01:14:02.790
I can't wait to read that kind of writing.

01:14:02.790 --> 01:14:04.803
It sounds like it's really fun.

01:14:04.803 --> 01:14:09.020
But I want to pick up on
something you just said

01:14:09.020 --> 01:14:11.800
and follow up on Kelvin's comment.

01:14:11.800 --> 01:14:13.010
You mentioned the legacies

01:14:13.010 --> 01:14:17.193
of pre-national forums of connectedness.

01:14:18.660 --> 01:14:23.520
And I'm just wondering,
in using, maybe we do need

01:14:23.520 --> 01:14:28.220
to theorize cosmopolitanism
more explicitly and better

01:14:28.220 --> 01:14:31.793
because it's a wonderful notion,

01:14:31.793 --> 01:14:35.795
but I wonder to what degree
it does kind of reinscribe

01:14:35.795 --> 01:14:38.550
the idea of a dichotomy between

01:14:39.860 --> 01:14:44.270
the internal autonomous,
authentic Southeast Asia thing

01:14:44.270 --> 01:14:49.040
versus this externally
generated cosmopolitanism idea

01:14:49.040 --> 01:14:52.523
or whatever it is, mercantile expansion,

01:14:54.068 --> 01:14:59.068
or various forms of connectedness
that have come outside in.

01:14:59.970 --> 01:15:03.120
Like, this is a false
dichotomy in my eyes.

01:15:03.120 --> 01:15:06.290
So I'm just wondering if
there's room in thinking

01:15:06.290 --> 01:15:10.420
about the precursors to
these cosmopolitan linkages

01:15:10.420 --> 01:15:15.230
for forms that maybe could
have started in Southeast Asia

01:15:16.180 --> 01:15:19.430
and simultaneously linked up
to these cosmopolitanisms.

01:15:19.430 --> 01:15:22.840
So that's one thing I wanted to mention.

01:15:22.840 --> 01:15:27.320
And then the other thing,
since I'm here is just,

01:15:27.320 --> 01:15:29.300
where were the women in these revolutions?

01:15:29.300 --> 01:15:33.153
Or was the role of women just
sociologically incidental?

01:15:36.422 --> 01:15:37.544
- Great questions.

01:15:37.544 --> 01:15:41.200
I can see three core questions there.

01:15:41.200 --> 01:15:44.670
The second I'm not fully sure I fully get,

01:15:44.670 --> 01:15:47.510
but let me try at least two of them

01:15:47.510 --> 01:15:49.860
and then get onto the middle one.

01:15:49.860 --> 01:15:52.160
So in terms of Takashi Shiraishi,

01:15:52.160 --> 01:15:56.320
he definitely is acknowledged
in the acknowledgements

01:15:56.320 --> 01:16:00.450
as an important influence given
the course I took with him

01:16:00.450 --> 01:16:03.410
as a graduate student
and the kind of panoramic

01:16:03.410 --> 01:16:07.540
comparative historical
sociologically-informed account

01:16:07.540 --> 01:16:10.920
he gave of modern Southeast Asian history.

01:16:10.920 --> 01:16:14.530
But also, as you mentioned,
"An Age in Motion"

01:16:14.530 --> 01:16:16.890
and that interest in Haji Misbach

01:16:16.890 --> 01:16:21.890
and in the Sarekat Islam and
so forth, which as you know,

01:16:22.700 --> 01:16:27.160
we'll see also is being revisited

01:16:27.160 --> 01:16:31.380
by other scholars these days as well.

01:16:31.380 --> 01:16:35.810
So I think that interest in
what did and didn't happen

01:16:35.810 --> 01:16:38.050
between Islam and communism is there.

01:16:38.050 --> 01:16:41.570
And in the book, I spent a lot
of time on the Sarekat Islam,

01:16:41.570 --> 01:16:46.570
and there's a lot of Takashi
there, so that's there.

01:16:46.840 --> 01:16:49.280
In terms of the question of gender

01:16:49.280 --> 01:16:50.809
and of women in particular,

01:16:50.809 --> 01:16:55.809
I think I belatedly realized
towards the end of the book

01:16:56.553 --> 01:17:00.230
that there's an argument that
I would like to bring out

01:17:00.230 --> 01:17:02.353
perhaps in an article

01:17:02.353 --> 01:17:05.880
that kind of teases it
out more explicitly.

01:17:05.880 --> 01:17:08.246
And that links back to this notion

01:17:08.246 --> 01:17:12.413
of scholastic cosmopolitanism.

01:17:14.340 --> 01:17:18.510
Insofar as these forms of pre-modern

01:17:18.510 --> 01:17:23.510
and early modern scholasticism
are overwhelmingly male,

01:17:24.850 --> 01:17:29.850
their survival or their
demise has implications

01:17:31.220 --> 01:17:35.470
not only in terms of politics
but also in terms of gender.

01:17:35.470 --> 01:17:39.353
And what I see as distinctive
in the Vietnamese Revolution

01:17:39.353 --> 01:17:44.353
is that the demise of this
kind of infrastructure

01:17:50.290 --> 01:17:55.290
by the 19 teens in
Vietnam opens up a field

01:17:56.893 --> 01:18:01.893
of educational experience
and socialization

01:18:02.899 --> 01:18:07.899
that doesn't have the kind
of male-only qualities

01:18:09.010 --> 01:18:11.130
and restrictions on it that we see

01:18:11.130 --> 01:18:14.560
in the Islamic schools and that we see

01:18:16.540 --> 01:18:21.070
in the late 19th century
Philippines heavily as well.

01:18:21.070 --> 01:18:24.810
And I think that if you look
at the Vietnamese Revolution,

01:18:24.810 --> 01:18:27.240
the role of women is really striking.

01:18:27.240 --> 01:18:29.340
I mean, we have so many different accounts

01:18:29.340 --> 01:18:33.834
of the role of women and
women hold up half the sky.

01:18:33.834 --> 01:18:37.310
The Vietnamese Revolution,
compared to what people

01:18:37.310 --> 01:18:42.310
have been able to show about
20th century Indonesian

01:18:42.870 --> 01:18:45.990
revolutionary or Filipino
left-wing politics,

01:18:45.990 --> 01:18:50.750
I think there's a much
more impressive history

01:18:50.750 --> 01:18:53.470
that shows that there
are implications here

01:18:53.470 --> 01:18:56.733
in terms of women's participation.

01:18:57.600 --> 01:19:00.260
So I think that's the story.

01:19:00.260 --> 01:19:03.100
And if people have said, well, you know,

01:19:03.100 --> 01:19:06.400
that the book suggests,
but it doesn't draw it out

01:19:06.400 --> 01:19:11.080
with the kind of evidence that
I think I could glom together

01:19:11.080 --> 01:19:15.050
for an article to make that
kind of comparative case

01:19:15.050 --> 01:19:17.250
more explicitly and extensively.

01:19:17.250 --> 01:19:21.040
But I think there's something
like that that is important

01:19:22.140 --> 01:19:25.880
in terms of the different implications

01:19:25.880 --> 01:19:29.796
of these different histories
for these institutions

01:19:29.796 --> 01:19:34.796
of reproducing patriarchy and
male forms of male monopoly

01:19:36.550 --> 01:19:37.870
on certain forms of knowledge,

01:19:37.870 --> 01:19:39.673
religious knowledge and so forth.

01:19:40.790 --> 01:19:45.593
In terms of the dangers of
a kind of cosmopolitanism

01:19:48.240 --> 01:19:51.290
at the expense of, like that, oh,

01:19:51.290 --> 01:19:53.106
all Southeast Asians are cosmopolitans

01:19:53.106 --> 01:19:57.914
or like a kind of unidirectional account.

01:19:57.914 --> 01:20:01.200
I mean, I guess in the
book what I'm trying to do

01:20:01.200 --> 01:20:04.710
is to suggest that the ways
in which these societies

01:20:04.710 --> 01:20:09.273
were constituted and
evolved had a lot to do

01:20:10.920 --> 01:20:14.227
with various different external elements.

01:20:14.227 --> 01:20:18.070
And what became Vietnamese,
what became Filipino,

01:20:18.070 --> 01:20:23.070
what became Indonesian had
outside and inside intertwined.

01:20:25.160 --> 01:20:28.620
So I think that's what
I'm trying to suggest

01:20:28.620 --> 01:20:31.050
and maybe didn't articulate it well.

01:20:31.050 --> 01:20:33.352
But I can see that I'm privileging

01:20:33.352 --> 01:20:36.233
the coasts over the interiors.

01:20:42.147 --> 01:20:46.963
There's a lot that's maybe
getting missed there, right?

01:20:48.860 --> 01:20:51.210
And so it could be the pendulum

01:20:51.210 --> 01:20:56.113
is swinging too far back in my account.

01:20:58.870 --> 01:21:02.120
And the proof has to be in
the Southeast Asian pudding

01:21:02.120 --> 01:21:06.160
in terms of, you know, I
guess my interest is less

01:21:06.160 --> 01:21:10.472
and my confidence is
less in an understanding

01:21:10.472 --> 01:21:15.470
of consciousness but rather
in some kind of sociology.

01:21:15.470 --> 01:21:19.660
And so if we ask ourselves
how the people we read about

01:21:19.660 --> 01:21:22.968
in Mary Steedly's account
of the Indonesian revolution

01:21:22.968 --> 01:21:27.080
or in accounts of these
little towns in Tonkin,

01:21:27.080 --> 01:21:31.823
in 1945, when they hear about independence

01:21:32.680 --> 01:21:34.946
being declared in Hanoi.

01:21:34.946 --> 01:21:39.070
How did these people, what
possibilities were there

01:21:39.070 --> 01:21:40.790
for organizing and mobilizing?

01:21:40.790 --> 01:21:42.980
And what possibilities were foreclosed

01:21:42.980 --> 01:21:46.000
sociologically, institutionally?

01:21:46.000 --> 01:21:48.220
That's what I feel more confident

01:21:48.220 --> 01:21:50.603
is knowable, at least for me.

01:21:53.950 --> 01:21:56.575
Yeah, okay, thank you.

01:21:56.575 --> 01:21:59.540
- Thanks for this stimulating discussion.

01:21:59.540 --> 01:22:03.010
Our last question will
be from Rianne Subijanto.

01:22:03.010 --> 01:22:04.130
And I want to apologize,

01:22:04.130 --> 01:22:08.980
I think I have misspelled
Rianne's name twice in my chat.

01:22:08.980 --> 01:22:11.770
My eyes can't read my
tiny screen very well.

01:22:11.770 --> 01:22:14.270
So apologies to you Rianne,

01:22:14.270 --> 01:22:16.020
but you can have the last question.

01:22:17.520 --> 01:22:19.763
- No problem, thank you so much.

01:22:20.700 --> 01:22:23.910
Hi, John, it's very nice
to meet you finally.

01:22:23.910 --> 01:22:28.381
- Same.
- And my question is,

01:22:28.381 --> 01:22:30.750
thank you so much for the book, obviously.

01:22:30.750 --> 01:22:35.720
It's brilliant and it
gives a fresh new pathways

01:22:37.750 --> 01:22:40.000
for research in Southeast Asia.

01:22:40.000 --> 01:22:43.650
And my question is on the
idea of cosmopolitanism.

01:22:43.650 --> 01:22:48.090
And I was thinking here, what
makes it a cosmopolitanism?

01:22:48.090 --> 01:22:52.150
And what you've mentioned
is cosmopolitanism

01:22:52.150 --> 01:22:54.940
is forms of connectedness
and infrastructure.

01:22:54.940 --> 01:22:56.615
So is it ideology?

01:22:56.615 --> 01:22:58.720
Because when we think about ideology,

01:22:58.720 --> 01:23:00.900
which is what you talk about also, right.

01:23:00.900 --> 01:23:02.200
Of course you talk about
the infrastructure.

01:23:02.200 --> 01:23:04.160
They are traces of nationalism

01:23:04.160 --> 01:23:07.360
or what I like to say,
parochialism instead of nationalism

01:23:07.360 --> 01:23:09.569
in Islam and communism, right?

01:23:09.569 --> 01:23:14.250
And also nationalism is very
much cosmopolitan in a sense

01:23:14.250 --> 01:23:17.839
that it is a template that's
become copied elsewhere.

01:23:17.839 --> 01:23:21.060
So I was thinking that maybe instead

01:23:21.060 --> 01:23:23.810
of thinking of cosmopolitanism
versus nationalism,

01:23:23.810 --> 01:23:26.751
we're thinking about
cosmopolitanism versus parochialism.

01:23:26.751 --> 01:23:28.860
And then what makes it cosmopolitanism,

01:23:28.860 --> 01:23:30.970
is it maybe, is it also material?

01:23:30.970 --> 01:23:32.730
Because then if we think of it as material

01:23:32.730 --> 01:23:36.000
in terms of shipping
lines, infrastructure,

01:23:36.000 --> 01:23:39.890
of context, media, and language,
then we can trace those

01:23:39.890 --> 01:23:42.940
in terms of the nature of those contexts

01:23:42.940 --> 01:23:45.190
because in the history
of Indonesian communism

01:23:45.190 --> 01:23:48.870
in the early 1920s, for example,

01:23:48.870 --> 01:23:53.870
during the underground time
after the revolt in 1927,

01:23:54.153 --> 01:23:56.700
'26, right, Djamaluddin Tamin

01:23:56.700 --> 01:24:00.610
and all of the other
fellow communist exiles,

01:24:00.610 --> 01:24:04.410
they went to Singapore and
Malaysia, but their network

01:24:04.410 --> 01:24:08.590
is very much among the
Muslim Malay people.

01:24:08.590 --> 01:24:10.870
It's not really with the, for example,

01:24:10.870 --> 01:24:15.409
the communist Chinese in Malaysia.

01:24:15.409 --> 01:24:19.480
So tracing that very specificity

01:24:21.150 --> 01:24:24.920
of the scope of cosmopolitanism
is very important.

01:24:24.920 --> 01:24:27.740
And then also, then this
is my second question,

01:24:27.740 --> 01:24:30.530
what is the characteristics,
or maybe origin,

01:24:30.530 --> 01:24:32.707
which I don't really like the word,

01:24:32.707 --> 01:24:34.603
but characteristics of cosmopolitanism

01:24:34.603 --> 01:24:36.655
that is specifically Southeast Asia?

01:24:36.655 --> 01:24:41.190
In other words, what is
Southeast Asian contribution

01:24:41.190 --> 01:24:43.143
to the history of cosmopolitanism?

01:24:46.150 --> 01:24:48.793
- More tough questions
about cosmopolitanism.

01:24:51.550 --> 01:24:55.797
I guess for me, if you look
at the root of the terms,

01:24:55.797 --> 01:25:00.797
you know, cosmos and
cosmopolitanism there seems to be

01:25:01.660 --> 01:25:05.390
a claim and an aspiration to something

01:25:05.390 --> 01:25:08.650
that transcends the specific, to some kind

01:25:08.650 --> 01:25:13.650
of world global universality
that one can identify

01:25:15.410 --> 01:25:20.410
in these different cases that is of course

01:25:20.410 --> 01:25:25.410
a form of denial, erasure,
suppression, disavowal

01:25:26.800 --> 01:25:31.800
of specificity, of
self-interest, of parochialisms.

01:25:32.815 --> 01:25:35.673
There is no view from nowhere, right?

01:25:36.920 --> 01:25:41.920
And so I think in that
sense, what I find noteworthy

01:25:42.800 --> 01:25:47.800
and perhaps appealing in these
different cosmopolitanisms

01:25:48.950 --> 01:25:53.483
in different ways and to
different extents is the seeds,

01:25:54.640 --> 01:25:57.770
at least the ones that
I focus on in the book

01:25:57.770 --> 01:26:02.770
in the revolutionary context
is they each have some degree

01:26:02.820 --> 01:26:07.820
of emancipatory and egalitarian promise.

01:26:09.350 --> 01:26:13.460
But they also have within
them something else.

01:26:13.460 --> 01:26:18.099
They also have within
them forms of domination,

01:26:18.099 --> 01:26:21.160
forms of patriarchy, forms of inequality,

01:26:21.160 --> 01:26:22.978
forms of exploitation.

01:26:22.978 --> 01:26:24.840
They have other things there, too.

01:26:24.840 --> 01:26:29.840
And they have the seeds
of state-making as well.

01:26:30.950 --> 01:26:35.460
But I think historically,
if you take the template,

01:26:35.460 --> 01:26:39.500
the template is the emergence
of these sorts of energies

01:26:39.500 --> 01:26:44.010
and forms of mobilization that
contribute to a revolution.

01:26:44.010 --> 01:26:47.270
And then after a revolution,
and we can see this

01:26:47.270 --> 01:26:49.860
after the Russian Revolution,
you can see this in art

01:26:49.860 --> 01:26:53.480
and literature and architecture
as much as you can see it

01:26:53.480 --> 01:26:57.023
in political terms, then
there's the etatization,

01:26:57.935 --> 01:27:02.470
the appropriation by the
state, by the nation state

01:27:02.470 --> 01:27:06.360
in the 20th century and
the kind of evisceration

01:27:06.360 --> 01:27:10.740
of those more ambiguous
emancipatory energies

01:27:12.020 --> 01:27:15.085
that are then appropriated or suppressed.

01:27:15.085 --> 01:27:17.450
And so maybe part of the project,

01:27:17.450 --> 01:27:20.050
to go back to some of the
earlier questions also,

01:27:20.050 --> 01:27:24.996
is to kind of, as with your
work, to kind of remind people

01:27:24.996 --> 01:27:29.920
of other times and other places
when these kinds of hopes

01:27:29.920 --> 01:27:32.221
and dreams inspired action

01:27:32.221 --> 01:27:35.443
that then was appropriated by others.

01:27:36.310 --> 01:27:39.840
And that some of those
emancipatory energies,

01:27:39.840 --> 01:27:41.410
many of those emancipatory energies

01:27:41.410 --> 01:27:46.343
were then absorbed and killed off.

01:27:48.007 --> 01:27:51.283
And the origins and energies
were then disavowed.

01:27:53.110 --> 01:27:54.960
And I don't know if that
fully answers your question.

01:27:54.960 --> 01:27:57.770
In terms of Southeast Asian contributions

01:27:57.770 --> 01:28:02.157
to cosmopolitanisms, I think it's clear

01:28:03.840 --> 01:28:06.460
that Southeast Asia has really contributed

01:28:06.460 --> 01:28:09.610
a great deal to globalization,

01:28:09.610 --> 01:28:11.890
to communist internationalism,

01:28:11.890 --> 01:28:15.850
to different forms of
revolutionary internationalism.

01:28:15.850 --> 01:28:18.810
And even in the more
conventional international,

01:28:18.810 --> 01:28:21.510
the kind of Bandung forms of solidarity.

01:28:21.510 --> 01:28:24.623
So I think, you know, I
begin the book by saying,

01:28:26.230 --> 01:28:31.230
when Che Guevara said in Havana in 1966,

01:28:31.587 --> 01:28:35.290
"We'll bring you one,
two, three more Vietnams,"

01:28:35.290 --> 01:28:37.150
everyone knew what he was talking about.

01:28:37.150 --> 01:28:39.020
He just had to say the word Vietnam

01:28:39.020 --> 01:28:41.163
and people knew what he meant.

01:28:42.871 --> 01:28:47.871
So I think that Southeast
Asia is an incredibly,

01:28:49.940 --> 01:28:52.890
just as it contributed in many ways

01:28:52.890 --> 01:28:56.520
to the study of nationalism
and to nationalist struggles,

01:28:56.520 --> 01:28:59.140
it also has contributed
to these sorts of forms

01:28:59.140 --> 01:29:02.813
of internationalism and
cosmopolitanism as well.

01:29:07.648 --> 01:29:09.330
- Thank you.

01:29:09.330 --> 01:29:10.970
- Thank you so much, John.

01:29:10.970 --> 01:29:12.530
We're now at the end of our time.

01:29:12.530 --> 01:29:15.670
I'm going to stop the record right now,

01:29:15.670 --> 01:29:19.350
and those who wish to linger
for informal chat can do so.

01:29:19.350 --> 01:29:20.993
So I'll click the button here.

